(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3.873a3da4da.js"]={"31":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which bacterium is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries?","choices":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica","D":"Campylobacter jejuni","E":"Shigella dysenteriae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-resource countries and an important cause of traveler's diarrhea and food poisoning, most often from improperly cooked chicken. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Campylobacter are comma-shaped, flagellated, gram-negative organisms diagnosed mainly by stool culture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection can be followed by reactive arthritis, mainly in HLA-B27–positive patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the known reservoir of Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Poultry","B":"Humans","C":"Shellfish","D":"Cattle","E":"Swine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Humans are the known reservoir of Shigella. Up to 10^9 organisms per gram of stool and an infective dose of several hundred organisms make fecal-oral spread by water and food very efficient. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella are gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonmotile facultative anaerobes closely related to enteroinvasive E. coli (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the United States, children in daycare, migrant workers, travelers, and nursing home residents are most affected (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal sites does Yersinia infection preferentially involve?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, sigmoid, and left colon","B":"Stomach, duodenum, and jejunum","C":"Ileum, appendix, and right colon","D":"Esophagus, stomach, and duodenum","E":"Jejunum, transverse colon, and rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yersinia infections preferentially involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Organisms proliferate in lymphoid tissue, causing Peyer patch and regional node hyperplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and contaminated water and tend to cluster in winter (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) are the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea and spread via contaminated food or water. In under-resourced countries, children younger than 2 years are particularly susceptible. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ETEC are noninvasive and produce heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: like cholera, ETEC infection produces mild histologic changes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and invades epithelial cells without producing toxins?","choices":{"A":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC) resemble Shigella bacteriologically. They do not produce toxins but invade epithelial cells and cause a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC infections are most common among young children in under-resourced countries (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EIEC are transmitted via food, water, or person-to-person contact (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What does the heat-stable toxin of ETEC bind on enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase","B":"GM1 ganglioside receptor","C":"Rho family GTPases","D":"Guanylate cyclase","E":"Beta-1 integrin receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ETEC heat-stable toxin (ST) binds guanylate cyclase and raises intracellular cGMP; its effects on epithelial ion transport resemble those of the heat-labile toxin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin resembles cholera toxin and activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and chloride secretion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC are noninvasive, so mucosal biopsies show mild change (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What type of organism is Tropheryma whipplei?","choices":{"A":"Gram-negative curved spirochete","B":"Gram-positive actinomycete","C":"Acid-fast mycobacterial rod","D":"Flagellated intestinal protozoan","E":"Gram-negative comma-shaped rod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Whipple's original postmortem found foamy macrophages filled with gram-positive microbes; the organism was identified as an actinomycete in 1992 and named Tropheryma whipplei. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tropheryma whipplei is an intracellular gram-positive organism (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: intact rod-shaped bacilli can be seen within macrophages by electron microscopy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which stain result best separates Whipple disease from intestinal mycobacterial infection?","choices":{"A":"Positive mucicarmine stain","B":"Positive Gram stain result","C":"Negative acid-fast stain","D":"Positive Congo red stain","E":"Negative Giemsa stain result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both Whipple disease and intestinal mycobacterial infection show foamy macrophages with PAS-positive organisms. Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive, while T. whipplei is not. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: mycobacterial infection can mimic Whipple disease, particularly in an immunocompromised host (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which demographic group is Whipple disease most common in?","choices":{"A":"East Asian women","B":"West African children","C":"Native American teens","D":"South Asian older women","E":"Northern European men"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Whipple disease is most common in males of Northern European descent, particularly farmers and others with occupational exposure to soil or animals. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease is most common in older males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which virus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide as rotavirus vaccine use has grown?","choices":{"A":"Norovirus","B":"Rotavirus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Astrovirus","E":"Sapovirus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With increasing rotavirus vaccine use, norovirus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide, responsible for about 20% of acute gastroenteritis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus is an icosahedral virus with a single-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infectious particles can be shed before symptoms and for up to 8 weeks afterward in healthy individuals (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which age range is most vulnerable to rotavirus?","choices":{"A":"Birth to 6 months","B":"2 to 5 years","C":"5 to 12 years","D":"Over 65 years","E":"6 to 24 months"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because breast-milk antibodies protect during the first 6 months of life. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is an nonenveloped virus with a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the WHO recommends the first rotavirus vaccine dose at 6 weeks of age (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the typical incubation period of adenovirus gastroenteritis?","choices":{"A":"About 12 hours","B":"About 2 days","C":"About 3 weeks","D":"About 1 week","E":"About 2 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenovirus disease typically presents after an incubation period of about 1 week, with diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal pain; symptoms generally resolve within 10 days. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea that also affects older immunocompromised patients (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: fever and weight loss may accompany adenovirus gastroenteritis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"How is giardiasis usually documented?","choices":{"A":"Modified acid-fast stool stain","B":"Colonic biopsy for trophozoites","C":"Stool cyst immunofluorescence","D":"Serum antiparasite IgG titers","E":"Stool culture on selective agar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Giardia infection is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples. Oral antimicrobial therapy is effective, but recurrence is common. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia trophozoites in duodenal biopsies are pear-shaped with two equally sized nuclei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infants, travelers, and immunocompromised patients have increased risk of severe giardiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis?","choices":{"A":"Oral vancomycin","B":"Albendazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Nitazoxanide","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation, and is the most effective treatment for systemic disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. histolytica cysts have a chitin wall that resists gastric acid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: amebiasis most often affects the cecum and ascending colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"What property lets Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated but unfiltered water?","choices":{"A":"Chlorine resistance","B":"Heat resistance","C":"Chitin acid shield","D":"Surface antigen variation","E":"Bile acid tolerance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are resistant to chlorine and persist in treated but unfiltered water; contaminated drinking water is the most common means of transmission. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is based on finding oocysts in the stool (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 1993 Milwaukee outbreak from inadequate water purification affected more than 400,000 people (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes 2 weeks after diarrhea from undercooked chicken. What process most likely links the two illnesses?","choices":{"A":"Molecular mimicry of gangliosides","B":"Direct invasion of nerve roots","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release","D":"Immune complexes in muscle fibers","E":"HLA-B27 linked joint inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter infection reflects molecular mimicry: antibodies to C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide cross-react with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides. It is not HLA-linked. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 40% of Guillain-Barré cases have documented Campylobacter infection in the prior 2 weeks (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman returns from the Philippines with fever, abdominal pain, and small salmon-colored macules on her chest. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods. What will her terminal ileum most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures","D":"Raised Peyer patches with oval ulcers","E":"Flattened villi with lymphocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Typhoid fever (S. typhi) invades small intestinal M cells. Peyer patches in the terminal ileum enlarge into plateau-like elevations, and mucosal damage creates oval ulcers oriented along the ileal axis that may perforate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Typhoid fever). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rose spots on the chest and abdomen are a feature of typhoid fever (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gallbladder colonization with S. typhi may be associated with gallstones and a chronic carrier state (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in daycare has fever, cramps, and bloody diarrhea, and stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Oral rehydration","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics","C":"Antimotility agents","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Intravenous saline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antidiarrheal medications can prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance, so they are contraindicated. Antibiotic treatment shortens both the clinical course and the duration of bacterial shedding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Shigellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella infections are most prominent in the left colon, and M-cell tropism can produce aphthous ulcers resembling Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Shigella dysenteriae serotype 1 releases Shiga toxin, which inhibits protein synthesis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — antidiarrheal drug classes and their adverse effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea after a cookout with undercooked hamburgers. A week later she is pale, platelets are 40,000/µL (reference range 150,000–450,000/µL), and creatinine is rising. Which bacterial product most directly causes these complications?","choices":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin","B":"Heat-stable toxin","C":"Dispersin protein","D":"Shiga-like toxin","E":"Lipopolysaccharide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EHEC O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef produces Shiga-like toxins and is more likely than non-O157 serotypes to cause bloody diarrhea, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ischemic colitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC lesions show an ischemia-like morphology with surface atrophy and erosion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: antibiotic treatment can release Shiga-like toxins and raise hemolytic uremic syndrome risk, especially in children (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-month-old boy in a crowded urban daycare has 5 days of watery diarrhea. Research biopsy shows bacteria tightly attached to enterocyte apical membranes with local effacement of microvilli. Which group is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) produce attaching and effacing lesions: bacteria attach tightly to small intestinal enterocyte apical membranes and cause local effacement of microvilli. EPEC are a major cause of diarrhea in children under 2 years. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: assays for the bacterial proteins that insert into enterocyte membranes allow molecular diagnosis of EPEC (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EPEC are prevalent worldwide and cause endemic diarrhea and outbreaks (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops watery diarrhea 3 days after arriving in Mexico. Stool testing identifies an E. coli strain producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. What would a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","B":"Ischemic surface erosion","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers","D":"Foamy lamina macrophages","E":"Minimal mucosal changes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ETEC are noninvasive and act through toxins that raise cAMP and cGMP. Like cholera, ETEC infection results in mild histologic changes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin activates adenylate cyclase, while the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC spread through contaminated food or water (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man who farms in rural Wisconsin has diarrhea, weight loss, joint pain, and memory problems. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. What will special staining most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Acid-fast bacilli in macrophages","B":"PAS-positive lysosomal granules","C":"Congo red apple-green deposits","D":"Prussian blue iron granules","E":"Mucicarmine-staining capsules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The macrophages of Whipple disease contain PAS-positive, diastase-resistant granules representing partially digested bacteria within lysosomes. T. whipplei is acid-fast negative, unlike mycobacteria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac symptoms, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms are common in Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the usual triad is diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man working on a dairy farm has steatorrhea, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria filled with foamy macrophages, and tissue PCR detects Tropheryma whipplei. What best explains his malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Impaired lymph drainage","B":"Brush-border enzyme loss","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","D":"Bile salt deconjugation","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages accumulate in the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes and obstruct lymphatics. The malabsorptive diarrhea results from impaired lymph drainage. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and steatorrhea occur later in the course of Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the rarity of infection despite many healthy carriers suggests genetic risk factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has had migratory joint pain for 2 years and now has diarrhea, weight loss, and a new heart murmur. Biopsy shows foamy macrophages expanding duodenal villi. What will endoscopy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions","C":"Yellow-white mucosal plaques","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dense macrophage infiltrates expand villi and give a shaggy surface. Lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition produce endoscopically visible white to yellow plaques. Macrophages can also accumulate in cardiac valves, synovium, and brain. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the shaggy mucosal surface reflects villous expansion by dense macrophage infiltrates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms such as arthritis may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman on a cruise ship develops vomiting and watery diarrhea during a shipwide outbreak and recovers in 2 days. Her 70-year-old husband, equally exposed, never becomes ill, and his saliva lacks blood group antigens. Which trait most likely protects him?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 positive genotype","B":"Heterozygous CFTR mutation","C":"Sickle hemoglobin trait carrier","D":"HLA-DQ2 haplotype expression","E":"Homozygous FUT2 inactivation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mutations inactivating FUT2 prevent fucosylation of blood group antigens that serve as norovirus binding sites. These homozygous nonsecretors are naturally resistant; about 20% of people of European and African descent carry this genotype. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Norovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus outbreaks spread in schools, hospitals, nursing homes, and cruise ships through droplets, surfaces, and fomites (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: an infective dose of fewer than 20 viral particles makes prevention difficult (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old unvaccinated boy has vomiting and watery diarrhea during a daycare outbreak. The virus has a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which viral factor may mediate the enterocyte damage?","choices":{"A":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase","B":"Nonstructural protein 4","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Spike glycoprotein","E":"Envelope fusion protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes; damage may be mediated by nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis. Immature cells then repopulate the villus surface. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is about 10 viral particles (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus vaccines are incompletely effective in under-resourced regions, possibly due to malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man on immunosuppression after a heart transplant has 6 days of diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Stool PCR detects adenovirus. What would a small intestinal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intranuclear viral inclusions","B":"Foamy macrophages in lamina propria","C":"Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adenovirus biopsies can show epithelial degeneration but more often show nonspecific villous atrophy and compensatory crypt hyperplasia. Viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Adenovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: adenovirus symptoms generally resolve within 10 days (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — villi and microvilli as absorptive surface area","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Mexico had bloody diarrhea a month ago and now has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a 9-cm liver abscess, and colon biopsy showed organisms ingesting red cells. How did the parasite most likely reach the liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent through bile ducts","B":"Lymphatic spread to liver","C":"Direct transmural extension","D":"Portal venous embolization","E":"Hepatic artery seeding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica invades crypts and burrows into the lamina propria, forming flask-shaped ulcers. Parasites penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver, producing abscesses in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic liver abscesses can exceed 10 cm and have a shaggy fibrinous lining with scant marginal inflammation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: metronidazole is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who drank stream water while camping has had intermittent bloating and greasy diarrhea for 4 months. Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped organisms with two nuclei on the brush border. Which parasite feature best explains her months of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Invasion into the lamina propria","B":"Chitin wall resisting acid","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites","E":"Variant surface protein switching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giardia evade immune clearance through continuous modification of their major surface antigen, variant surface protein, and can persist for months or years with intermittent symptoms. They do not invade. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Giardia lamblia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia decrease brush-border enzyme expression and cause microvillous damage and epithelial apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Giardia cysts resist chlorine, so infection is endemic in unfiltered public water supplies (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with advanced HIV has weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Ileal biopsy shows small blue spheres that appear to sit on the brush border, and stool shows oocysts. Where does the organism actually reside?","choices":{"A":"Free in lumen over villi","B":"Vacuole within enterocytes","C":"Inside lamina propria macrophages","D":"Within submucosal lymphatics","E":"Inside flask-shaped ulcers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cryptosporidium sporozoites attach to the brush border and induce enterocytes to engulf them into an endocytic vacuole, so the intracellular parasite appears to sit on the apical membrane. Sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and tight junction leak cause watery diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Cryptosporidium). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptosporidium causes sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and increased tight junction permeability, producing watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in immunodeficient hosts, organisms can involve the biliary and respiratory tracts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What do endoscopic and microscopic evaluations show in irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosal findings","B":"Patchy crypt abscesses","C":"Thickened collagen band","D":"Diffuse villous blunting","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite real symptoms, endoscopic and microscopic evaluations are normal in IBS, so the diagnosis rests on clinical symptoms and functional testing. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS has no structural abnormalities and is most common in middle-aged females (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS is divided into diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, and mixed subtypes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of conditions is associated with irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sacroiliitis and uveitis","B":"Gallstones and kidney stones","C":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis","D":"Fibromyalgia and depression","E":"Pyoderma and episcleritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Elentra objective lists fibromyalgia and mood disorders as IBS associations, and First Aid notes IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and mood disorders such as anxiety and depression. The distractors are extraintestinal or metabolic complications of inflammatory bowel disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and with anxiety or depression (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: candidate IBS genes include serotonin reuptake transporters and cannabinoid receptors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucocorticoid course","B":"Lifestyle and diet changes","C":"Long-term opioid therapy","D":"Empiric antibiotic course","E":"Elective subtotal colectomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elentra objective and First Aid both give lifestyle modification and dietary changes as first-line IBS treatment. Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease, and colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-line IBS treatment is lifestyle modification and dietary change (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: 5-HT3 receptor antagonists are effective in many cases of diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene encoding an intracellular sensor of bacterial muramyl dipeptide is most strongly associated with Crohn disease?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"CFTR","C":"NOD2","D":"HFE","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NOD2 encodes an intracellular sensor of muramyl dipeptide and is the gene most strongly associated with Crohn disease in European populations, though risk alleles occur in about 30% of patients. NOD2 variants are protective in ulcerative colitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATG16L1 and IRGM, which act in autophagosome formation, are additional Crohn disease risk genes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: very early onset IBD can be monogenic, most often from IL10 or IL-10 receptor mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which form of microscopic colitis shows a strong association with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Collagenous colitis","B":"Diversion colitis","C":"Ischemic colitis","D":"Ulcerative proctitis","E":"Lymphocytic colitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphocytic colitis is strongly associated with celiac disease and autoimmune diseases including Graves disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and autoimmune gastritis. Its collagen layer is of normal thickness, and intraepithelial lymphocytes often exceed 20 per 100 colonocytes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis is most common in older females and has a normal endoscopic appearance (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: both forms of microscopic colitis cause chronic, nonbloody, watery diarrhea without weight loss (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibodies are often present in Crohn disease but typically absent in ulcerative colitis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies","B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease patients often have anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies, which are typically absent in ulcerative colitis. In contrast, up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients have p-ANCA. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease may be positive for anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: fecal calprotectin helps distinguish IBD activity from noninflammatory disease such as IBS (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colonic wall do sigmoid diverticula form?","choices":{"A":"Bases of colonic crypts","B":"Lymphoid aggregate sites","C":"Vasa recta entry points","D":"Mesenteric fat attachments","E":"Haustral fold crests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Focal gaps in the inner circular muscle occur where nerves and arterial vasa recta penetrate it. In the colon these gaps are unreinforced, so raised intraluminal pressure herniates mucosa through them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur especially where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: colonic diverticula are 0.5 to 1 cm flask-like outpouchings that are uniformly spaced (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of Western adults older than 60 years have colonic diverticular disease?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic diverticula are rare before age 30, but prevalence approaches 50% in Western adults older than 60 years. They are less common in Japan and under-resourced countries, probably from dietary differences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with obesity and diets low in fiber and high in total fat or red meat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: diverticula tend to occur in the right colon in Asian and African populations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the sex ratio of Meckel diverticula?","choices":{"A":"Twice as common in males","B":"Twice as common in females","C":"Equal in males and females","D":"Four times more in females","E":"Ten times more in males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticula have a male-to-female ratio of 2:1, occur in about 2% of people, lie within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, are about 2 inches long, and are most often symptomatic by age 2. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Meckel diverticulum extends from the antimesenteric side of the ileum and may contain ectopic pancreatic tissue (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: about 4% of Meckel diverticula ever become symptomatic (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colon are hyperplastic polyps most commonly found?","choices":{"A":"Right colon","B":"Transverse colon","C":"Cecum and appendix","D":"Left colon","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are most common in the left colon, are typically less than 5 mm, and are frequently multiple in the sigmoid colon and rectum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are predominantly located in the rectosigmoid region (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colonic hyperplastic polyps are typically discovered in the sixth and seventh decades (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed pathogenesis of colonic hyperplastic polyps?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic APC gene inactivation","B":"Germline STK11 gene mutation","C":"Repeated mucosal prolapse injury","D":"Mismatch repair gene silencing","E":"Delayed epithelial shedding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are thought to result from decreased epithelial turnover and delayed shedding of surface epithelial cells, leading to a piling up of goblet and absorptive cells. Mucosal prolapse drives solitary rectal ulcer polyps instead. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells with a serrated surface architecture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: epithelial hyperplasia can occur as a nonspecific reaction overlying any mass and may signal an adjacent lesion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What underlying abnormality causes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prior pelvic radiation for carcinoma","B":"Poor anorectal sphincter relaxation","C":"Hypoperfusion of the rectal watershed","D":"Recurrent enteric bacterial infection","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impaired relaxation of the anorectal sphincter creates a sharp angle at the anterior rectal shelf, causing recurrent abrasion and ulceration of the overlying mucosa. Entrapment of the resulting polyp in the fecal stream leads to mucosal prolapse. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer syndrome presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and an inflammatory lesion of the anterior rectal wall (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: inflammatory polyps form through repeated cycles of injury and healing (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is the most common mutation identified in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"KRAS","C":"MUTYH","D":"SMAD4","E":"PTEN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SMAD4, which encodes a signaling intermediate in the TGF-beta pathway, is the most common mutation in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. BMPR1A mutations account for other cases, and together they explain fewer than half of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis is an autosomal dominant syndrome of numerous hamartomatous polyps in the colon, stomach, and small bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: patients with SMAD4 mutations often have both juvenile polyposis and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Where are the polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Small intestine","C":"Rectosigmoid colon","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are most common in the small intestine, although they may occur in the stomach and colon and rarely in the bladder and lungs. Grossly they are large, pedunculated, and lobulated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome features numerous hamartomatous polyps throughout the GI tract with hyperpigmented macules (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome presents at a median age of 11 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway does the Peutz-Jeghers tumor suppressor STK11 regulate?","choices":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"AMPK signaling","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Hedgehog signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"STK11 is a tumor suppressor that regulates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) pathways, which control cell polarization and brake growth and anabolic metabolism. Germline heterozygous loss-of-function STK11 mutations occur in about half of familial cases. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: the second normal STK11 copy is often lost through somatic mutation in Peutz-Jeghers-associated cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has crampy lower abdominal pain on about 5 days each month for 6 months, with bloating and alternating loose and hard stools. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which additional feature best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools","B":"Weight loss over 10%","C":"Elevated stool calprotectin","D":"Pain eased by defecation","E":"Symptom onset after age 60"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists IBS criteria as abdominal pain or discomfort at least 3 days per month over 3 months with improvement after defecation and a change in stool frequency or form, after other causes are excluded. An elevated fecal calprotectin points toward organic disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome, Inflammatory bowel diseases). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is recurrent abdominal pain with at least 2 of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form, so under FA no single feature is mandatory (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS prevalence in high-income countries is about 5% to 10%, peaking between 20 and 40 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility, haustral contractions, and mass movements","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has 2 years of urgent loose stools and intermittent abdominal cramping. Colonoscopy is normal, and he does not have celiac disease. A test shows excess fecal bile acids. Which mechanism best explains this subtype of his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury","B":"Subepithelial collagen band","C":"Bile acid malabsorption","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess bile acid synthesis or bile acid malabsorption is one identified cause of diarrhea-predominant IBS, likely through effects of bile acids on intestinal motility and epithelial ion transport. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea-predominant IBS shows increased colonic contractions and transit rates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: opioids and anticholinergic psychoactive drugs are also used for diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman returns from Mexico after a week of fever and watery diarrhea. Six months later she has recurring cramps, bloating, and variable stool form with normal colonoscopy and biopsies. Which mechanism is proposed for this onset pattern?","choices":{"A":"Shift in the gut microbiome","B":"Antibodies to enteric neurons","C":"Persistent mucosal granulomas","D":"Chronic bacterial tissue invasion","E":"Subepithelial collagen deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some IBS patients relate onset to a bout of infectious gastroenteritis, suggesting that immune activation or a shift in the gut microbiome triggers disease. Granulomas, invasion, and collagen deposition would produce abnormal biopsies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is chronic and may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: defective brain-gut axis signaling is proposed in IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 8 months of right lower quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a draining perianal tract. Resection of a strictured terminal ileum shows a thick rubbery wall with deep fissures. Which additional gross finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous disease of mucosa","B":"Thin wall with smooth serosa","C":"Superficial broad ulcers","D":"Flat atrophic mucosal surface","E":"Creeping fat over the serosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In transmural Crohn disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface as creeping fat. Wall thickening from edema, fibrosis, and muscular hypertrophy drives strictures. Continuous mucosal disease with a normal serosa fits ulcerative colitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease shows cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and a string sign on small bowel follow-through (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: disease often recurs at the anastomosis, and up to 40% need more resection within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with newly diagnosed ulcerative colitis has pruritus and an alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (reference range 44–147 U/L). MRCP shows beaded intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. When should colonoscopic dysplasia surveillance begin?","choices":{"A":"About 8 years after diagnosis","B":"At the time of diagnosis","C":"Beginning at 50 years of age","D":"After the first bloody relapse","E":"Upon confirmation of pancolitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with IBD typically enter surveillance about 8 years after diagnosis. The major exception is IBD with primary sclerosing cholangitis, which carries greater cancer risk, so surveillance starts at diagnosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease) (Colitis-associated neoplasia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: colitis-associated cancer risk rises sharply 8 to 10 years after onset (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholestatic disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has 4 months of watery, nonbloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is normal, but biopsies show more than 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes with a collagen layer of normal thickness. Her medications are listed. Which is most likely contributing?","choices":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Levothyroxine","D":"Naproxen","E":"Amlodipine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These biopsies show lymphocytic microscopic colitis. Similar histologic changes can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis histology shows lamina propria lymphocytes with intraepithelial lymphocytosis or a thickened subepithelial collagen band (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: collagenous colitis has a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — large-intestine lamina propria and the collagen layer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man had left lower quadrant pain and fever with a WBC of 16,000/µL (reference range 4,500–11,000/µL) 2 months ago. He now has recurrent urinary tract infections and air in his urine. Which complication best explains his current findings?","choices":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Ischemic stricture","D":"Free perforation","E":"Colovesical fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inflammation in sigmoid diverticula can extend through the thin wall to cause serositis, abscesses, sinus tracts, and perforation. A tract into the bladder produces pneumaturia and recurrent infection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis complications include abscess, colovesical fistula with pneumaturia, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: surgery is generally reserved for severe or recurrent diverticulitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a lifelong low-fiber diet has dozens of sigmoid outpouchings on colonoscopy, but small bowel imaging shows no outpouchings. Which structural difference best explains the sparing of her small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Lower intraluminal small bowel pressure","B":"Absence of penetrating vasa recta","C":"Denser submucosal lymphoid tissue","D":"Continuous longitudinal muscle coat","E":"Thicker muscularis mucosae layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Both bowels have gaps in the inner circular muscle where nerves and vasa recta penetrate. In the small intestine these gaps are reinforced by a continuous outer longitudinal layer, whereas in the colon that layer is gathered into taeniae coli. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is caused by intraluminal pressure and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: hypertrophy of the inner circular muscle is commonly associated with diverticulosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy has episodes of screaming with legs drawn up, vomiting, and a stool of blood and mucus. A sausage-shaped right abdominal mass is palpated. At surgery, a congenital ileal pouch lined by gastric mucosa is found at the leading point. Which complication of this pouch explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diverticulitis","B":"Intussusception","C":"Small bowel volvulus","D":"Peptic perforation","E":"Adhesive obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Meckel diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception and can also cause hematochezia, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause painless hematochezia, RLQ pain, intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: symptomatic Meckel cases can mimic acute appendicitis or obstruction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps. Histology shows mature goblet and absorptive cells with a saw-tooth outline limited to the upper third of the crypts. Which premalignant lesion must be distinguished from these?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Juvenile hamartoma","C":"Sessile serrated polyp","D":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","E":"Lymphoid aggregate polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, but they must be distinguished from sessile serrated polyps, which are histologically similar and premalignant. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps show a saw-tooth crypt pattern, BRAF mutations, and CpG island methylation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps may occur singly but are frequently multiple (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with 15 years of ulcerative pancolitis has colonoscopy showing many mucosal projections, some joined at their tips into bridges. Biopsies show no dysplasia. How do these projections form?","choices":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth","B":"Clonal adenomatous epithelial growth","C":"Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands","E":"Regrowth of mucosa between ulcers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ulcerative colitis, isolated islands of regenerating mucosa bulge into the lumen to create pseudopolyps, and their tips may fuse into mucosal bridges. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory pseudopolyps are due to mucosal erosion in inflammatory bowel disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: pseudopolyps are marked in ulcerative colitis and moderate in Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and chronic straining. A polypoid lesion is on the anterior rectal wall. Biopsy shows erosion and epithelial hyperplasia. Which additional histologic feature indicates mucosal prolapse?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria smooth muscle","B":"Noncaseating submucosal granulomas","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen","D":"Apoptotic bodies in crypt bases","E":"Foamy macrophages in lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome polyps show mixed inflammation, erosion, and epithelial hyperplasia with prolapse-induced fibromuscular hyperplasia of the lamina propria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer polyps are an example of an inflammatory lesion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: granulation tissue-like capillary proliferation in the lamina propria reflects repeated erosion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding, and a red mass prolapses through the anus. The resected pedunculated polyp is smooth, with cystic glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris in an expanded lamina propria. He has no family history of polyps. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","B":"Juvenile retention polyp","C":"Tubulovillous adenoma","D":"Sessile hyperplastic polyp","E":"Submucosal leiomyoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps, also called retention polyps, are usually solitary, often rectal, and occur mostly before age 5. They show cystic mucin-filled glands in inflamed lamina propria and can prolapse through the anal sphincter. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary hamartomatous polyps do not have significant risk of transformation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: dysplasia is rare in sporadic juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has dozens of hamartomatous polyps in the colon, recurrent nosebleeds, lip telangiectasias, and a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. Her father had similar polyps. Which malignancy is she at greatest risk of developing by her mid-40s?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","C":"Colonic adenocarcinoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic islet tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with dysplasia in polyps and separate adenomas, and 30% to 50% of patients develop colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations are recognized extraintestinal features. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with increased risk of colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colectomy may be needed to limit chronic hemorrhage from ulcerated juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has dark blue-brown macules on his lips, nostrils, and palms, and imaging shows multiple GI polyps. A resected polyp has an arborizing network of smooth muscle and normal epithelium. Which neoplasm warrants surveillance starting at birth?","choices":{"A":"Sex cord stromal tumor","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Small intestinal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma","E":"Lung adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries a lifetime cancer risk near 40%. Surveillance begins at birth for testicular sex cord tumors, in late childhood for gastric and small intestinal cancers, and in the second and third decades for colon, pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian, and uterine cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries increased risk of breast and GI cancers, including colorectal, gastric, small bowel, and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: smooth muscle mixed with lamina propria distinguishes Peutz-Jeghers polyps from juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true}],"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic hallmark defines colorectal adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Crypt base serration","B":"Mucosal prolapse fibrosis","C":"Epithelial dysplasia","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Hamartomatous smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hallmark of colorectal adenomas is epithelial dysplasia, seen as nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and stratification, often with prominent nucleoli and fewer goblet cells. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are neoplastic and arise via the chromosomal instability pathway with APC and KRAS mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colorectal adenomas range from 0.3 to 10 cm and have a velvet or raspberry-like surface (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of colorectal adenomas larger than 4 cm contain foci of invasive cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 40 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy risk in an adenoma. Cancer is rare in adenomas under 1 cm, while nearly 40% of lesions larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous histology carries more malignant potential than tubular histology, and tubulovillous is intermediate (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular adenomas, partly because they tend to be larger (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does intramucosal carcinoma within an adenoma carry little metastatic potential?","choices":{"A":"Absent mucosal lymphatics","B":"Intact muscularis propria","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional lymphatic channels are absent in the colonic mucosa, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential and complete polypectomy is generally curative. Invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae carries a risk of spread. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomas are usually asymptomatic but may present with occult bleeding (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: invasion into the submucosal stalk of a pedunculated polyp constitutes invasive adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"How many adenomas are needed for a diagnosis of classic familial adenomatous polyposis?","choices":{"A":"At least 10","B":"At least 25","C":"At least 100","D":"At least 50","E":"At least 1000"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At least 100 polyps are necessary for a diagnosis of classic FAP, though thousands may be present. Apart from their number, FAP polyps look like sporadic adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP polyps arise after puberty, are pancolonic, and involve the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: about 75% of FAP cases are inherited and the remainder arise from de novo mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the protein product of the gene mutated in familial adenomatous polyposis normally do?","choices":{"A":"Repairs base mismatches","B":"Activates RAS signaling","C":"Inhibits mTOR signaling","D":"Blocks TGF beta signals","E":"Degrades beta catenin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"APC is a key negative regulator of Wnt signaling. It binds and promotes degradation of beta catenin, so its loss lets beta catenin enter the nucleus and drive MYC and cyclin D1 transcription. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP is an autosomal dominant mutation of the APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancers without APC mutations often carry beta catenin mutations that resist APC-dependent degradation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extraintestinal manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis can be detected at birth?","choices":{"A":"Multiple mandibular osteomas","B":"Abdominal wall desmoid tumors","C":"Multiple epidermoid skin cysts","D":"Retinal pigment hypertrophy","E":"Impacted supernumerary teeth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium can be detected at birth and may be an adjunct to early FAP screening. Osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental anomalies appear later in variants such as Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gardner syndrome adds osseous and soft tissue tumors and impacted or supernumerary teeth to FAP (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: specific APC mutations partly explain FAP variants (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which genes are mutated in most patients with Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"APC or CTNNB1","B":"BRCA1 or BRCA2","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A","D":"KRAS or BRAF","E":"MSH2 or MLH1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There are at least five mismatch repair genes, but most HNPCC patients have mutations in MSH2 or MLH1. Patients inherit one mutant and one normal allele. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant mismatch repair defect with about 80% progression to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: the human genome has about 50,000 to 100,000 microsatellites (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of all colorectal cancers are attributed to hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 10 to 15 percent","B":"About 2 to 4 percent","C":"About 20 to 25 percent","D":"About 40 to 50 percent","E":"About 70 to 80 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HNPCC accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers, making it the most common syndromic form of colon cancer. Its cancers occur at younger ages than sporadic colon cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is associated with endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: HNPCC patients have fewer polyps and develop cancer later than FAP patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which colonic region is characteristically involved in Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Rectal ampulla","C":"Proximal colon","D":"Splenic flexure","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The objective and First Aid place Lynch syndrome in the proximal colon, and Robbins notes HNPCC colon cancers are often in the right colon. Sources: SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3; Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal colon is involved in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: microsatellite unstable and CIMP tumors are frequently located in the right colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"At what age does colorectal cancer incidence peak?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 years","B":"30 to 40 years","C":"40 to 50 years","D":"60 to 70 years","E":"80 to 90 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colorectal cancer incidence peaks at 60 to 70 years of age. Fewer than 20% of cases occur before age 50, though incidence before age 40 is rising. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most colorectal cancer patients are older than 50 years, and about 25% have a family history (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: colorectal adenocarcinoma causes nearly 15% of US cancer deaths, second to lung cancer (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern is most closely associated with increased colorectal cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Low vegetable fiber diet","B":"High citrus fruit intake","C":"Low red meat consumption","D":"High fish oil consumption","E":"Low refined sugar intake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low intake of unabsorbable vegetable fiber with high intake of refined carbohydrates and fat is most closely tied to colorectal cancer. Reduced stool bulk may prolong mucosal contact with bacterial oxidative byproducts. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include adenomatous and serrated polyps, IBD, tobacco use, and processed meat with low fiber (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high fat intake raises hepatic bile acid synthesis, and bacteria can convert bile acids into carcinogens (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which organ is the most common site of distant metastasis from colon cancer?","choices":{"A":"Lung","B":"Liver","C":"Bone","D":"Brain","E":"Ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the colon drains through the portal circulation, the liver is the most common site of distant metastases. Regional nodes, lungs, and bones can also be involved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectosigmoid is the most common colorectal cancer site, followed by ascending and descending colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the rectum does not drain via the portal circulation, so metastatic anal carcinomas often circumvent the portal route (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Deep tenderness two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine is known by what name?","choices":{"A":"Rovsing sign","B":"Psoas sign","C":"Murphy sign","D":"McBurney sign","E":"Obturator sign"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The McBurney sign is deep tenderness two-thirds of the distance from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, a classic finding in acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute appendicitis may also elicit psoas, obturator, and Rovsing signs (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: acute appendicitis has a lifetime risk of 7% and affects males slightly more often (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common tumor of the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous cystadenoma","B":"Signet ring carcinoma","C":"Burkitt lymphoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common appendiceal tumor. It is usually incidental, forms a bulbous swelling at the distal tip, and rarely spreads. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are used to treat carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Octreotide). COMLEX: conventional adenomas and adenocarcinomas of the appendix can cause obstruction that mimics acute appendicitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major risk factor for HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","choices":{"A":"Receptive anal intercourse","B":"Chronic laxative overuse","C":"Portal venous hypertension","D":"Low dietary fiber intake","E":"Untreated ulcerative colitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anal squamous cell carcinoma is frequently associated with high-risk HPV strains, and the major risk factor for these HPV-associated lesions is anal sex. Low-risk HPV strains cause condyloma acuminatum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Anal canal). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma arises below the pectinate line, while adenocarcinoma arises above it (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: basaloid anal squamous cell carcinoma has been called cloacogenic carcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile colonic polyp with long, slender surface projections on biopsy. Her daughter asks what best predicts whether such a growth harbors cancer. Which feature is most important?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell number","B":"Degree of inflammation","C":"Lesion diameter","D":"Tubular gland pattern","E":"Patient sex and age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy in an adenoma. Villous architecture correlates with cancer partly because villous adenomas are larger, and architectural category has little significance in isolation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: tubular adenomas tend to be small pedunculated polyps of rounded glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has three colonic adenomas removed. One shows high-grade dysplasia and the others show low-grade dysplasia. How does the high-grade lesion affect cancer risk in his remaining polyps?","choices":{"A":"Higher risk in other polyps","B":"Unchanged risk in other polyps","C":"Field cancerization of colon","D":"Germline repair defect implied","E":"Doubled risk in other polyps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-grade dysplasia is a risk factor for cancer in that individual polyp but does not increase cancer risk in other polyps within that patient. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps progress through an adenoma-carcinoma sequence with APC loss followed by KRAS mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high-grade dysplasia raises cancer risk within the individual polyp (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — chronic reflux injury and Barrett dysplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has small adenomas removed at each of several surveillance endoscopies. She asks why repeated procedures lower her colon cancer risk. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"Ablation of mucosal lymphatics","B":"Reversal of APC mutations","C":"Induction of mucosal immunity","D":"Removal of precursor polyps","E":"Shrinkage of hyperplastic polyps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenomas are precursors of colorectal adenocarcinoma, and removed polyps do not progress to adenocarcinoma, so repeated surveillance is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Most adenomas do not progress, and no tool identifies those that will. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk screening begins at age 45 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: adenomas develop in about 30% of Western adults by age 60 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with familial adenomatous polyposis had a prophylactic colectomy at age 20. Which site most needs continued endoscopic surveillance for adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Distal esophagus","C":"Gallbladder fundus","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Hepatic hilum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colectomy prevents colorectal cancer in FAP, but patients remain at risk elsewhere. The ampulla of Vater and the stomach are common extracolonic sites of adenomas and, if untreated, cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without prophylactic colectomy, 100% of FAP patients progress to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: untreated FAP leads to colorectal adenocarcinoma often before age 30 and in nearly all untreated patients by age 50 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 4 — location-dependent presentation of pancreatic head and periampullary tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man has about 30 colonic adenomas plus several serrated and hyperplastic polyps. His parents have normal screening exams, but his brother has a similar polyp burden. APC testing is negative. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"SMAD4","C":"PTEN","D":"MSH6","E":"MUTYH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is an autosomal recessive base-excision repair disorder in patients without APC loss. It has fewer than 100 polyps that appear later, with frequent sessile serrated and hyperplastic polyps. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MUTYH-associated polyposis is also associated with duodenal adenomas and ovarian and bladder cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancer development in MUTYH-associated polyposis is delayed relative to FAP (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with numerous colonic adenomas also has firm bony masses of the skull and a fibrous mass in the abdominal wall. Which variant best describes her disease?","choices":{"A":"Turcot syndrome","B":"Cowden syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Gardner syndrome","E":"Li Fraumeni syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is an APC-related FAP variant with multiple adenomas plus osteomas, thyroid and desmoid tumors, and skin cysts. Turcot syndrome adds CNS tumors such as medulloblastoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis) (Table 17.10). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome is FAP or Lynch syndrome plus a malignant CNS tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: attenuated FAP is also caused by APC mutation (Robbins Ch. 17, Table 17.10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 5 — hamartomatous polyposis syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 45, and tumor staining shows loss of a mismatch repair protein. Which mutational mechanism most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"Chromosomal instability","B":"Telomere attrition crisis","C":"Oncogene amplification","D":"Whole chromosome aneuploidy","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In HNPCC, loss of the second mismatch repair allele raises mutation rates up to 1000-fold, mostly in short repeats called microsatellites. Chromosomal instability is the hallmark of the APC/beta catenin pathway. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome mismatch repair defects cause subsequent microsatellite instability (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: MSI can target TGFBR2 and BAX, promoting growth and survival of abnormal clones (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with no family history of cancer has a mucinous large bowel adenocarcinoma with peritumoral lymphocytes and mismatch repair deficiency. Which finding best supports a sporadic origin rather than a hereditary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous differentiation","B":"Advanced patient age","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes","D":"BRAF activating mutation","E":"Repair protein loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sporadic mismatch repair–deficient cancers often arise through the CpG island hypermethylation pathway, which silences MLH1. The combination of MSI, BRAF mutation, and methylation is the signature of this pathway. Mucinous change, lymphocytes, and right-sided location occur in both. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps follow the CIMP pathway, which may silence MLH1 and lead to BRAF mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: KRAS and TP53 are typically intact in CIMP cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman's brother had colon cancer at 38 and her mother had endometrial cancer at 50. Genetic testing confirms a germline mismatch repair mutation. Which other cancer is she at increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian carcinoma","B":"Breast carcinoma","C":"Thyroid carcinoma","D":"Lung carcinoma","E":"Cervical carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HNPCC clusters cancers of the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin. The objective names endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3. Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Lynch syndrome mnemonic CEOS stands for colon, endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: identifying germline mismatch repair mutations matters for genetic counseling and second malignancy risk (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference 12 to 16), MCV is 72 fL (reference 80 to 100), and ferritin is 8 ng/mL (reference 15 to 150). She denies visible bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy","B":"Colonoscopy referral","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis","D":"Empiric oral iron","E":"Serum B12 level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers often present with fatigue and weakness from iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency anemia in an older male or postmenopausal female is GI cancer until proven otherwise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in males older than 50 and postmenopausal females raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: proximal colon tumors grow as polypoid masses that rarely obstruct (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has months of narrowing stool caliber, crampy left lower quadrant pain, and bloating. Imaging shows a short constricting sigmoid mass. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sessile serrated plaque","B":"Large pedunculated villous polyp","C":"Diffuse linitis plastica","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa","E":"Annular napkin-ring lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distal colon carcinomas tend to be annular napkin-ring constrictions that narrow the lumen, sometimes to obstruction. Left-sided tumors produce visible bleeding, bowel habit change, and left lower quadrant cramping. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: left-sided colorectal cancer is associated with hematochezia and obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: invasive colon cancer elicits a desmoplastic response responsible for its firm consistency (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man takes daily low-dose aspirin after a myocardial infarction and asks whether it affects his colon cancer risk. Which mechanism most likely explains a protective effect?","choices":{"A":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","B":"Thromboxane receptor blockade","C":"Cyclooxygenase 2 inhibition","D":"Platelet P2Y12 blockade","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs are protective, likely through inhibition of COX-2, which is highly expressed in 90% of colorectal carcinomas and 40% to 90% of adenomas. COX-2 drives prostaglandin E2, which promotes epithelial proliferation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: overexpression of COX-2 has been linked to colorectal cancer, and NSAIDs may be chemopreventive (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some NSAIDs cause polyp regression in FAP patients whose rectum was left in place (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs and H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower quadrant, then nausea and low-grade fever. His resected appendix is examined. Which histologic finding is required for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria neutrophils","B":"Mucosal neutrophils with ulcers","C":"Lymphoid follicle hyperplasia","D":"Submucosal eosinophil clusters","E":"Granulomas in lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are not specific. Diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can be due to fecalith obstruction in adults or lymphoid hyperplasia in children (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, abscess, and bacteremia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic constipation and straining notices bright red blood on toilet tissue. Anoscopy shows dilated anal venous cushions. Which condition must still be excluded?","choices":{"A":"Anal fistula","B":"Pilonidal cyst","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Perianal abscess","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hemorrhoids present with pain and bright red blood on toilet tissue, but colon cancer can coexist and must be excluded. Straining at defecation and venous stasis of pregnancy are the main predisposing influences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hemorrhoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids receive visceral innervation and are not painful (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: hemorrhoidal bleeding can be treated by sclerotherapy, rubber band ligation, or infrared coagulation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has progressive abdominal distention. Surgery finds tenacious, semisolid mucin filling the peritoneal cavity and a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix with pushing tumor margins. Which primary lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Appendiceal carcinoid tumor","B":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasm","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma","E":"Gastric signet ring cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasms occur in adults, most often in the sixth decade, with pushing margins and extracellular mucin that can rupture the appendix. Peritoneal spread causes pseudomyxoma peritonei. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal spread of appendiceal mucinous neoplasms can produce pseudomyxoma peritonei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: GNAS, encoding Gαs, is frequently mutated in appendiceal mucinous neoplasms and is thought to drive excess mucin production (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — mucinous pancreatic cystic neoplasms","isHighYield":true}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most severely in kwashiorkor?","choices":{"A":"Somatic protein compartment","B":"Bone mineral compartment","C":"Plasma lipid compartment","D":"Intracellular water compartment","E":"Visceral protein compartment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor results when protein deprivation is relatively greater than the reduction in total calories, and it causes marked loss of the visceral protein compartment, chiefly the liver, with resulting hypoalbuminemia. Marasmus predominantly depletes the somatic compartment of skeletal muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Protein Energy Malnutrition). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kwashiorkor mnemonic MEALS lists malnutrition, edema, anemia, liver malfunction, and skin lesions (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: serum albumin and transferrin reflect the visceral protein compartment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Marasmus is defined when a child's weight falls to about what percentage of normal for sex, height, and age?","choices":{"A":"About 30 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 45 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins defines marasmus as weight falling to about 60% of normal for sex, height, and age, with growth retardation and loss of muscle and subcutaneous fat. In kwashiorkor, weight is typically 60% to 80% of normal, and the true loss is masked by edema. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: in marasmus the head appears large relative to the emaciated body (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"The enlarged fatty liver in kwashiorkor results mainly from which process?","choices":{"A":"Increased delivery of dietary fat","B":"Hepatic venous congestion","C":"Hepatocyte iron accumulation","D":"Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis","E":"Excess hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In kwashiorkor the liver is enlarged and fatty because synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins is reduced, so triglyceride is not exported efficiently. The diet is typically carbohydrate based rather than fat rich. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes the fatty change in kwashiorkor to decreased apolipoprotein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: superimposed fatty change can occur in marasmus as well, but it is characteristic of kwashiorkor (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Acute vitamin A toxicity produces headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that can mimic which condition?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Pseudotumor cerebri","C":"Migraine with aura","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute toxicity from a large dose of vitamin A causes headache, dizziness, vomiting, stupor, and blurred vision, symptoms that may be confused with a brain tumor, known as pseudotumor cerebri. Chronic toxicity causes weight loss, anorexia, nausea, and bone and joint pain. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Toxicity). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin A excess causes idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and isotretinoin is teratogenic (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: retinoic acid stimulates osteoclast production and activity, so chronic excess raises fracture risk (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin K is required for hepatic carboxylation of which group of clotting factors?","choices":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII","B":"Factors VIII, IX, XI, and XII","C":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X","D":"Factors II, V, VIII, and X","E":"Factors III, VII, XI, and XIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of procoagulant factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Deficiency produces a bleeding diathesis, especially in fat malabsorption and after broad antibiotic courses. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and lowers functional factor levels (First Aid Hematology, Warfarin). COMLEX: vitamin K is fat soluble, so biliary and pancreatic disease reduce its absorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor stimulates renal 1-alpha hydroxylase to increase active vitamin D production?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Fibroblast growth factor 23","C":"Serum hypercalcemia","D":"Serum hyperphosphatemia","E":"Calcitriol excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After hepatic 25-hydroxylation, the kidney converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and inhibited by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and FGF23. Hypocalcemia acts indirectly by raising PTH. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Metabolism). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease reduces renal activation of vitamin D (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the major circulating form and the usual marker of vitamin D status (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Riboflavin functions in the body after conversion to which coenzyme?","choices":{"A":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate","D":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Riboflavin is converted to flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, which serve as cofactors for many enzymes in intermediary metabolism. Deficiency causes cheilosis, stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis, and corneal vascularization. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization, the two Cs of B2 (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B2). COMLEX: niacin forms the nicotinamide coenzymes, while thiamine forms the pyrophosphate (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Copper is an essential component of which enzyme needed for collagen cross-linking?","choices":{"A":"Lysyl oxidase","B":"Glutathione peroxidase","C":"Carbonic anhydrase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Copper is a component of cytochrome c oxidase, dopamine beta hydroxylase, tyrosinase, lysyl oxidase, and other enzymes. Deficiency causes muscle weakness, neurologic defects, and abnormal collagen cross-linking, and it may follow excess zinc intake. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menkes disease impairs copper transport and lysyl oxidase activity (First Aid Biochemistry, Menkes disease). COMLEX: glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not copper (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Selenium deficiency is the cause of which endemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":{"A":"Wet beriberi","B":"Chagas disease","C":"Keshan disease","D":"Kawasaki disease","E":"Loeffler endocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Selenium is a component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme. Deficiency causes myopathy and congestive cardiomyopathy known as Keshan disease, described in regions with selenium-poor soil. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutathione peroxidase is a free radical scavenging enzyme (First Aid Pathology, Free radical injury). COMLEX: the antioxidant role of selenium overlaps functionally with vitamin E (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"The central processing system that integrates peripheral signals of energy balance lies mainly in which hypothalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Supraoptic nucleus","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"Preoptic nucleus","D":"Arcuate nucleus","E":"Mammillary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes energy balance as an afferent system, a central processing system in the hypothalamus, and an efferent system. Within the arcuate nucleus, POMC and CART neurons promote catabolism and NPY and AgRP neurons promote anabolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Regulation of Energy Balance). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a ventromedial hypothalamic lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: arcuate NPY and AgRP neurons also inhibit neighboring POMC neurons (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Peptide YY and glucagon-like peptide 1 are secreted by which intestinal cell type?","choices":{"A":"K cells","B":"I cells","C":"S cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptide YY and GLP-1 are secreted by L cells in the distal ileum and colon. GIP comes from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum. These gut signals act on the hypothalamus and pancreas after a meal. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are the incretin hormones of the small intestine (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Gastrointestinal regulatory substances). COMLEX: incretins act on pancreatic beta cells after enteral glucose (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Binding of GLP-1 to its receptor on pancreatic beta cells increases which second messenger to promote insulin release?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incretin receptors on beta cells are G protein coupled receptors that activate adenylate cyclase. The resulting rise in cyclic AMP enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose (First Aid Endocrine, Insulin). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 are the two principal incretins (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Gallstones are about how many times more common in obese individuals than in lean individuals?","choices":{"A":"About two times","B":"About six times","C":"About three times","D":"About ten times","E":"About twenty times"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallstones are six times more common in obese than in lean individuals. Obesity increases total body cholesterol turnover and biliary cholesterol excretion, which promotes cholesterol stone formation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Gallbladder Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol stones are linked to obesity, female sex, and estrogen (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Cholelithiasis). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol supersaturation is the key event in cholesterol stones (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity is associated with about what share of all cancers diagnosed in the United States?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that about 40% of all cancers diagnosed in the United States are associated with being overweight or obese. Gastrointestinal sites include the esophagus, upper stomach, colorectum, gallbladder, liver, and pancreas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obesity raises esophageal adenocarcinoma risk through reflux and Barrett esophagus (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Esophageal cancer). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia and adipokine changes link obesity to cancer (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity raises free estrogen in part by reducing hepatic synthesis of which protein?","choices":{"A":"Thyroxine binding globulin","B":"Corticosteroid binding globulin","C":"Vitamin D binding protein","D":"Retinol binding protein","E":"Sex hormone binding globulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity increases estrogen synthesis from androgens in adipose tissue and reduces hepatic synthesis of sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen. This mechanism helps explain the higher risk of endometrial and breast cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aromatase in adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen (First Aid Reproductive, Estrogen). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia also suppresses sex hormone binding globulin (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy in a refugee camp was weaned after his sister was born and now eats mainly maize gruel. He is apathetic and has generalized pitting edema, peeling hyperpigmented skin patches, pale banded hair, and hepatomegaly. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change most directly produces his edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","B":"Increased endothelial permeability","C":"Obstructed lymphatic drainage","D":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child has kwashiorkor. Loss of visceral protein lowers serum albumin, which reduces plasma oncotic pressure and allows fluid to leave capillaries, producing generalized edema. The flaky paint dermatosis and banded hair are characteristic accompanying findings. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a small child with a swollen belly and peeling skin is the classic kwashiorkor picture (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: kwashiorkor is also seen with protein-losing enteropathy, nephrotic syndrome, and burns (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had little food of any kind for months. She is emaciated with wasted limbs, no subcutaneous fat, and a head that looks large for her body. There is no edema. Her weight is 55% of expected for age. Which hormonal change most likely contributes to the loss of her fat stores?","choices":{"A":"Increased cortisol secretion","B":"Increased leptin secretion","C":"Increased insulin secretion","D":"Decreased glucagon secretion","E":"Decreased epinephrine secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This child has marasmus. Low leptin levels stimulate the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and the resulting high cortisol contributes to lipolysis. Fat and muscle are mobilized as energy sources. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus results from overall calorie deficiency rather than isolated protein lack (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus also causes immune deficiency, especially of T cell mediated immunity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy is treated for protein malnutrition with generalized edema, skin desquamation, and a fatty liver. After starting full-strength milk-based feeds he develops bloating and watery diarrhea. Stool is acidic and positive for reducing substances. Which mechanism most likely explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency","B":"Mucosal disaccharidase loss","C":"Cow milk protein allergy","D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, small bowel crypt mitoses decrease, causing mucosal atrophy, loss of villi and microvilli, and secondary disaccharidase deficiency. Infants may therefore respond poorly to full-strength milk diets until the mucosa recovers. Acidic stool with reducing substances points to undigested sugar. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactase deficiency gives osmotic diarrhea with low stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the mucosal changes of kwashiorkor reverse with treatment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 120 cm of distal ileum resected 3 years ago. She has greasy stools and now has trouble seeing when driving at dusk. Examination shows dry conjunctivae with small gray foamy plaques. The deficient vitamin normally serves which function?","choices":{"A":"Cofactor for gamma carboxylation","B":"Membrane lipid antioxidant","C":"Regulator of calcium absorption","D":"Component of visual pigment","E":"Cofactor for collagen hydroxylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin A deficiency develops in fat malabsorption such as Crohn disease because absorption requires bile and lipid digestion. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin, so night blindness appears first, followed by xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Deficiency). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bitot spots and night blindness indicate vitamin A deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: vitamin A deficiency also causes squamous metaplasia of airway and urinary epithelium (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — terminal ileal disease and bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis rarely takes her pancreatic enzymes. She has a wide-based gait, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with MCV 88 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Serum methylmalonic acid is normal. Which process most likely underlies her findings?","choices":{"A":"Myelin methylation failure","B":"Clotting factor undercarboxylation","C":"Membrane lipid peroxidation","D":"Collagen hydroxylation failure","E":"Rhodopsin regeneration failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects membrane lipids from free radical peroxidation. Deficiency, seen with fat malabsorption such as pancreatic insufficiency, causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. A normal methylmalonic acid argues against B12 deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin E). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 neuropathy without megaloblastosis or raised methylmalonic acid (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin E). COMLEX: vitamin E prevents oxidation of unsaturated fats in cell membranes (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with poorly controlled celiac disease has diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L), and PTH 98 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). A bone biopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization","B":"Mosaic pattern of lamellar bone","C":"Marrow fibrosis with brown tumors","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae","E":"Thickened seams of unmineralized osteoid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D malabsorption leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which restores calcium toward normal but leaves phosphate low. Impaired mineralization in the adult produces osteomalacia, in which excess unmineralized osteoid accumulates on bone surfaces. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Deficiency States). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteomalacia shows low calcium or phosphate with high PTH and alkaline phosphatase (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Osteomalacia). COMLEX: the same defect in children causes rickets with growth plate deformities (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with confusion and receives an IV dextrose infusion. Hours later he has nystagmus, bilateral lateral gaze palsy, and a wide-based gait. Serum lactate is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 4.5–19.8 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most directly explains his lactate rise?","choices":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase","C":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","D":"Fructose bisphosphatase","E":"Phosphofructokinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the coenzyme for oxidative decarboxylation reactions, including pyruvate dehydrogenase. Without it, pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and a glucose load worsens the deficit. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion indicate Wernicke encephalopathy. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Thiamine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: give thiamine before dextrose in alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B1). COMLEX: thiamine deficiency also causes dry and wet beriberi (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a metastatic midgut neuroendocrine tumor with episodic flushing and diarrhea. Over months she develops a rough dermatitis on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and new forgetfulness. Diversion of which amino acid most likely explains her new findings?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine","B":"Histidine","C":"Methionine","D":"Phenylalanine","E":"Tryptophan"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan toward serotonin, reducing niacin synthesis and producing pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Sources: First Aid Biochemistry (Vitamin B3: niacin from tryptophan; carcinoid syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pellagra occurs with carcinoid syndrome, Hartnup disease, and isoniazid use (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B3). COMLEX: niacin forms NAD and NADP for redox reactions (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has received home parenteral nutrition for 6 months using a trace element mix that omitted zinc, with other trace elements supplied after extensive small bowel resection. He now has a red scaly rash around his mouth, nose, and anus, worsening diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste. Which additional finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired night vision","B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zinc deficiency from zinc-free parenteral nutrition causes a periorificial rash like acrodermatitis enteropathica, anorexia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and impaired night vision. Zinc is a component of many enzymes, principally oxidases. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zinc deficiency causes delayed wound healing, dysgeusia, and hypogonadism (First Aid Biochemistry, Zinc). COMLEX: acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form of zinc malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has had insatiable hunger and severe obesity since infancy. Serum leptin is undetectable, and sequencing shows a homozygous mutation in the leptin gene. She begins daily recombinant leptin injections. The expected decrease in her food intake is mediated mainly by which hypothalamic effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of NPY and AgRP neurons","B":"Activation of lateral orexin neurons","C":"Activation of lateral MCH neurons","D":"Inhibition of paraventricular TRH neurons","E":"Activation of POMC and CART neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin acts in the arcuate nucleus to stimulate POMC and CART neurons, which release alpha MSH to act on melanocortin receptors and decrease food intake. It also inhibits NPY and AgRP neurons that drive feeding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin is secreted by adipose tissue and decreases appetite (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: mutations of leptin or its receptor cause massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Six months later she reports much less hunger before meals than she had with prior diets. A blunted rise of which hormone most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Cholecystokinin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is an orexigenic gut hormone that increases food intake. It is produced mainly in the stomach, rises before meals, and acts on NPY and AgRP neurons. Its preprandial rise is blunted after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, which may contribute to weight loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome and with sleep deprivation (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: ghrelin levels are typically lower in obese individuals (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 34 starts a weekly injected drug that mimics a hormone of ileal and colonic endocrine cells. Over 6 months his hemoglobin A1c improves and he loses 9 kg. Which action of the drug most likely accounts for his weight loss?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption","B":"Central suppression of food intake","C":"Increased brown fat thermogenesis","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion","E":"Decreased hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GLP-1 from intestinal L cells is an incretin that augments insulin release, and it also acts on the hypothalamus to decrease food intake and slows gastric emptying. GLP-1 receptor agonists produce weight loss mainly through reduced appetite. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Costanzo Ch. 8 (Hunger and Satiety).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonists decrease appetite and slow gastric emptying (First Aid Endocrine, Diabetes mellitus therapy). COMLEX: lipase inhibitors reduce fat absorption, a different weight loss mechanism (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 37 has acanthosis nigricans and a waist circumference of 118 cm. Fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL), and HDL 32 mg/dL (reference range: 40–60 mg/dL). Which mediator, released after inflammasome activation by excess free fatty acids, most likely drives his insulin resistance?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 10","B":"Interleukin 4","C":"Transforming growth factor beta","D":"Interleukin 1","E":"Interferon alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obesity, excess free fatty acids and lipid within cells activate the inflammasome, which releases interleukin 1 and creates a systemic proinflammatory state. This inflammation promotes insulin resistance, a core feature of the metabolic syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic syndrome combines central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia (First Aid Endocrine, Metabolic syndrome). COMLEX: obesity is associated with high triglycerides and low HDL (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 and type 2 diabetes drinks alcohol rarely. ALT is 78 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L) and AST is 52 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and viral and autoimmune studies are negative. A reduced serum level of which adipose hormone most likely contributes to her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Adiponectin","B":"Leptin","C":"Resistin","D":"Angiotensinogen","E":"Testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease. Adiponectin, which is reduced in obesity, increases fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity in liver and muscle. Its decline contributes to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hepatic steatosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Adiponectin; Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASLD is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with ALT usually above AST (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity related steatosis may progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a BMI of 39 and fasting hyperinsulinemia is diagnosed with sigmoid adenocarcinoma. His physician explains that high insulin raises free levels of a growth factor that stimulates tumor cell proliferation. Through which signaling route does this growth factor mainly act?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and catenin pathways","B":"RAS and PI3K pathways","C":"Notch and Hedgehog pathways","D":"TGF beta and SMAD pathways","E":"JAK and STAT pathways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperinsulinemia raises free insulin-like growth factor 1, a mitogen whose receptor activates the RAS and PI3K AKT pathways and promotes growth of tumor cells. This is one proposed link between obesity and colorectal and other cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include obesity, red meat, and a low fiber diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: excess adipose estrogen production is another obesity cancer link (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma risk factors","isHighYield":true}],"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Strongyloides stercoralis larvae enter the human host?","choices":{"A":"Penetration of intact skin","B":"Ingestion of eggs in soil","C":"Bite of a sandfly vector","D":"Inhalation of soil dust","E":"Ingestion of raw fish meat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides worms live in soil, and humans are infected when larvae penetrate the skin. The larvae travel in the circulation to the lungs, move up the trachea, and are swallowed. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides, Ancylostoma, and Necator enter through the skin, while Enterobius, Ascaris, Toxocara, Trichinella, and Trichuris are ingested (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides occurs sporadically in the United States, including in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do humans acquire Ascaris lumbricoides?","choices":{"A":"Ingestion of metacercariae on plants","B":"Ingestion of eggs passed in stool","C":"Ingestion of cysts in pond water","D":"Bites of infected deer flies","E":"Inhalation of larvae in soil dust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs from soil contaminated with human stool (A. lumbricoides) or pig stool (A. suum). Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris is spread fecal-orally, and its knobby-coated oval eggs are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: about a billion people are estimated to be infected with Ascaris (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"Humans acquire the adult intestinal form of Taenia solium by ingesting what?","choices":{"A":"Eggs in fecally soiled water","B":"Metacercariae on water plants","C":"Cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Cercariae in fresh lake water","E":"Eggs in undercooked raw beef meat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ingested cysticerci in undercooked pork attach to the intestinal wall and develop into adult tapeworms several meters long. This route completes the life cycle without causing disseminated cysticercosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm comes from encysted larvae in pork, and cysticercosis comes from eggs in food contaminated with human feces (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: tapeworm proglottids contain both male and female reproductive organs (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"For most intestinal helminths, which factor determines the adult worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Number of infective exposures","B":"Rate of adult worm replication","C":"Host gastric acid output","D":"Duration of fecal egg shedding","E":"Peak blood eosinophil count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most intestinal helminths do not multiply to new adults inside the human host, so each adult worm reflects a separately acquired infective egg or larva. Strongyloides is the key exception. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides hyperinfection reflects accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris symptoms vary with the burden of worms, and light infections are often asymptomatic (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"In strongyloidiasis, what allows the worm burden to rise without new exposure from soil?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms dividing by fission","B":"Eggs hatching in moist soil","C":"Larvae maturing within the host","D":"Cysts forming in skeletal muscle","E":"Proglottids budding off the scolex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most Strongyloides larvae pass in stool, but some develop into infective forms within the host, leading to autoinfection. Females in the small intestinal mucosa produce eggs by parthenogenesis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform Strongyloides larvae are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: immunocompetent hosts with Strongyloides are often asymptomatic but may have diarrhea, bloating, or malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class both suppresses eosinophil function and stimulates female Strongyloides to produce more infective larvae?","choices":{"A":"Beta blockers","B":"Antihistamines","C":"Statin agents","D":"Loop diuretic agents","E":"Corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils and other immune cells that accumulate in response to infection, and they stimulate female worms to increase larva production. Prolonged therapy can lead to fatal hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ivermectin or bendazoles treat Strongyloides (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: organ transplantation and lymphoma also increase the risk of hyperinfection (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"How do hookworms cause anemia?","choices":{"A":"Hemolysis of circulating red cells","B":"Blood loss from the intestinal wall","C":"Marrow invasion by migrating larvae","D":"Competition for dietary cobalamin","E":"Malabsorption of dietary folate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ancylostoma and Necator attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic blood loss and microcytic anemia. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia is the classic parasite hint for Ancylostoma and Necator (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae penetrate skin after walking barefoot on contaminated soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nutrient deficiency results from Diphyllobothrium latum infection?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Folic acid","C":"Thiamine","D":"Vitamin B12","E":"Ascorbic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia. Robbins notes that in humans it lives only in the gut and does not form cysticerci. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diphyllobothrium latum is acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the beef tapeworm and fish tapeworm are acquired by eating undercooked meat or fish (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can mechanically obstruct which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal valve","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Lower esophageal sphincter","D":"Internal anal sphincter","E":"Duodenojejunal flexure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can cause abdominal obstruction requiring surgery, including obstruction of the ileocecal valve and the appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may also cause biliary obstruction and intestinal perforation (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult Ascaris worms are 15 to 35 cm long and live mainly in the jejunum (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Th2 cytokine stimulates eosinophils in helminth infections?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 2","B":"Interleukin 12","C":"Interleukin 17","D":"Interferon gamma","E":"Interleukin 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Invasive helminths stimulate a Th2 response. IL-5 stimulates eosinophils, and IL-4 drives IgE production. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Filariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils produce major basic protein and respond to IL-5 (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: Th2 responses increase intestinal contractility, which expels adult Trichinella worms in animal models (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"When is peripheral eosinophilia most expected during a helminth infection?","choices":{"A":"When adults reach peak fecundity","B":"When eggs pass in the stool","C":"When worms attach by a scolex","D":"When larvae invade host tissues","E":"When adults reside in the cecum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eosinophilia is driven by Th2 responses to parasites that migrate through or encyst in tissue. Trichinella larvae invading muscle cause marked eosinophilia, and Ascaris larvae in the lungs cause eosinophilic pneumonitis. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites lead the PACMAN mnemonic for causes of eosinophilia (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: granulomas around schistosome eggs contain eosinophils, which are distinctive for helminth infections (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which eosinophil granule product is toxic to helminths?","choices":{"A":"Cathelicidin peptide","B":"Major basic protein","C":"Serum amyloid A","D":"Lysozyme enzyme","E":"Lactoferrin protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein, a helminthotoxin, along with eosinophil cationic protein and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin. Sources: First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils have bilobate nuclei and large uniform eosinophilic granules (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils that accumulate in tissues in response to Strongyloides (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"How does pyrantel pamoate eliminate susceptible intestinal nematodes?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of tubulin polymerization","B":"Increased tegument calcium permeability","C":"Neuromuscular blockade of worms","D":"Inhibition of parasite folate synthesis","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pyrantel is a poorly absorbed neuromuscular blocking agent that releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, paralyzing luminal worms, which are then expelled. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Pyrantel Pamoate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrantel pamoate is listed with ivermectin, mebendazole, and diethylcarbamazine as antihelminthic therapy (First Aid Microbiology (Antihelminthic therapy)). COMLEX: pyrantel is ineffective against Trichuris and Strongyloides infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q26","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"What shape do Enterobius vermicularis eggs have on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Barrel shape with polar plugs","B":"Oval with one flattened side","C":"Round with a thick knobby shell","D":"Oval with a large lateral spine","E":"Operculated with a terminal knob"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm eggs have a characteristic elongated oval shape with one of the long sides of the oval flattened. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm causes anal pruritus worse at night and is most common in children aged 5 to 10 (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult pinworms live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q27","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice treats intestinal infection with the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Diethylcarbamazine","D":"Pyrantel pamoate","E":"Mebendazole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung lists praziquantel or niclosamide as the drugs of choice for Diphyllobothrium latum and Taenia saginata, which live only in the gut lumen. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tapeworms such as Diphyllobothrium latum are covered with the cestodes (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: a single 2-g dose of niclosamide cures more than 85% of Diphyllobothrium latum infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old pig farmer passes a 25-cm worm in his stool. He recalls a dry cough with fever several weeks earlier. After the eggs were ingested, which route did the larvae take before maturing in his intestine?","choices":{"A":"Portal flow to the hepatic lobules","B":"Lymphatic flow to the dermis","C":"Direct growth in the jejunal lumen","D":"Lung passage then reswallowing","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested Ascaris eggs release larvae in the small intestine. The larvae penetrate the cecal mucosa, travel to the lungs in blood or lymph, mature for 10 to 14 days, and are coughed up and swallowed. Ascaris suum is acquired from pig stool. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: migration of Ascaris larvae to alveoli causes Löffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old vegetarian woman who emigrated from rural Guatemala has new seizures. MRI shows multiple small cystic brain lesions. How did she most likely acquire the infection?","choices":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork","B":"Metacercariae on watercress","C":"Eggs from dog feces","D":"Cercariae in lake water","E":"Eggs from human feces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs in food or water contaminated with human feces, oncospheres penetrate the gut wall and spread hematogenously to form cysticerci, including in the brain. Adult tapeworms do not form by this route. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brain cysts with seizures is the parasite hint for neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: viable cysticerci evade immunity with taeniaestatin and paramyosin (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker waded in a freshwater lake in East Africa. Months later she has hepatomegaly and eggs in her stool. Which mechanism allowed the parasite to enter her body?","choices":{"A":"Larval proteolytic enzymes","B":"Ingestion of snail tissue","C":"Bite of an infected black fly","D":"Inhalation of aerosolized eggs","E":"Ingestion of contaminated watercress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Schistosome cercariae swim in fresh water and penetrate human skin with proteolytic enzymes that degrade the keratinized layer, causing minimal skin reaction. S. mansoni affects mainly the liver and gut. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Schistosomiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension is the parasite hint for Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum (First Aid Microbiology (Trematodes); Parasite hints). COMLEX: females produce hundreds to thousands of eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has nocturnal perianal itching that returns weeks after one course of treatment. He often sucks his thumb, and no one else at home is affected. What best explains his reinfection?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated drinking water","B":"Hand-to-mouth egg transfer","C":"Adult worm division in cecum","D":"Insect vector bite","E":"Eating undercooked meat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm females migrate out of the anus at night to deposit eggs on perianal skin. The eggs become infectious within about 6 hours and spread from the hands or bed linens, allowing self-reinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is spread fecal-orally and diagnosed with the tape test (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm is the most common helminthic infection in the United States (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man from rural Kentucky takes high-dose prednisone after a lung transplant. He develops diffuse pulmonary infiltrates and Escherichia coli bacteremia, and larvae are found in his sputum. What is the source of the bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated central venous line","B":"Aspiration of gastric contents","C":"Larval damage to the bowel wall","D":"Infected urinary catheter","E":"Obstructed common bile duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Strongyloides hyperinfection, larvae invade the intestinal wall, and intestinal bacteria enter the blood through the damaged wall, causing sepsis. Worms of all stages may be found in the lungs and sputum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hyperinfection larvae invade the colonic submucosa, lymphatics, and blood vessels (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from Jamaica with no history of immunosuppressive drug use develops overwhelming strongyloidiasis with larvae throughout his body. Which underlying infection most likely predisposed him?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C virus","B":"Latent Epstein-Barr virus","C":"High-risk papillomavirus","D":"Reactivated human cytomegalovirus","E":"Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human T-lymphotropic virus type I infection predisposes to very high Strongyloides worm burdens through uncontrolled autoinfection, leading to fatal disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinfection syndrome results from accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical South America, sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl in rural Colombia passed a long roundworm last month. She now has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, and imaging shows a worm in the common bile duct. Which complication has developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute pancreatitis","B":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","D":"Splenic infarction","E":"Ureteral colic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ascaris worms that enter the biliary tree can cause acute pancreatitis and cholangitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may cause biliary obstruction and can emerge from the nose or mouth (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris infections are diagnosed by eggs in the stool or passed adult worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia who walks barefoot has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and MCV is 68 fL (reference 77–95 fL). Stool shows nematode eggs. Which additional laboratory finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low serum folate","B":"Low serum ferritin","C":"High indirect bilirubin","D":"High methylmalonic acid","E":"Positive Coombs test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hookworms suck blood from the intestinal wall, and chronic blood loss causes iron deficiency with microcytic anemia and depleted iron stores. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hookworm can also cause cutaneous larva migrans, a pruritic serpiginous rash (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae enter through skin after walking barefoot on contaminated beach or soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with nightly perianal itching develops vulvar itching and discharge. A tape test is positive. What mechanism explains her new symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Eggs carried in the bloodstream","B":"Larvae entering through the skin","C":"Bacteria spreading from the bladder","D":"Worms moving into the vagina","E":"Cysts forming in the vulvar tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterobius adults live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night. Worms moving into the vagina can cause vulvovaginitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is most common in children aged 5 to 10 years (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: very high pinworm burdens rarely cause rectal prolapse (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — appendicitis and anal canal lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He has fever, myalgias, and periorbital edema, and the eosinophil fraction is 32% (reference range: 1–4%). Where are the parasites now located?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hepatic sinusoids","C":"Lumen of the cecum","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trichinella larvae disseminate hematogenously and penetrate striated muscle cells, causing fever, myalgias, marked eosinophilia, and periorbital edema. The infected muscle cell gains a collagenous capsule and new blood vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle after undercooked pork ingestion (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: humans are dead-end hosts for Trichinella (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has dry cough, wheezing, fever, and transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia. Two stool examinations show no eggs, but 2 months later he passes a large roundworm. Why were the early stool tests negative?","choices":{"A":"Eggs were destroyed by acid","B":"Larvae had not yet matured","C":"Worms were in the bile duct","D":"Infection was in the skin","E":"Eggs were shed in urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial pulmonary stage of ascariasis causes Loeffler syndrome, a self-limited eosinophilic pneumonitis. Stool examination may not reveal the parasites if they have not yet matured in the intestine. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löffler syndrome is pulmonary eosinophilia from Ascaris larvae in the alveoli (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: heavy Ascaris burdens can cause malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old Ethiopian man who eats raw beef passes motile flat segments in his stool. The eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What best explains the normal eosinophil count?","choices":{"A":"Steroid suppression of eosinophils","B":"Concurrent bacterial sepsis","C":"Adult worm confined to gut lumen","D":"Eosinophils trapped in spleen","E":"Low parasite egg output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, Taenia saginata lives only in the gut lumen and does not form tissue cysticerci, so it lacks the tissue phase that drives strong Th2 eosinophilic responses. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites are a classic cause of eosinophilia in the PACMAN mnemonic (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: tapeworm mature distal proglottids contain many eggs and detach into the feces (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has 10 days of perianal itching that wakes her at night. A morning tape test is positive for pinworm eggs. Her CBC is normal, with hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mebendazole now and at 2 weeks","B":"Niclosamide as one single dose","C":"Oral ivermectin daily for 2 days","D":"Oral metronidazole for 7 days","E":"Oral vancomycin given for 10 days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tape test eggs flattened on one side confirm Enterobius. Katzung gives mebendazole 100 mg once for pinworm, repeated at 2 weeks, because reinfection from newly hatched worms is common. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm eggs are visualized via the tape test and treated with bendazoles or pyrantel pamoate (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm eggs mature rapidly and become infectious in about 6 hours (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from rural Kentucky has 3 months of intermittent diarrhea and bloating. His eosinophils are 14% (reference range: 1–3%). Stool microscopy shows motile larvae; no ova are seen. Which mechanism explains the action of the first-line drug for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Blocked tubulin polymerization in cells","B":"Intensified GABA signaling in nerves","C":"Increased calcium influx in tegument","D":"Blocked folate synthesis in cytoplasm","E":"Inhibited cell wall synthesis in worms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Larvae rather than ova in stool indicate Strongyloides stercoralis, whose larvae are mostly passed in the stool. Its drug of choice, ivermectin, paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission in peripheral nerves. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform larvae of Strongyloides are seen in feces under the microscope (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical regions and occurs sporadically in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy who recently moved from rural Bangladesh passes a 20-cm pink worm from his rectum. Stool examination shows numerous eggs. His hemoglobin is 11.9 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which drug is first-line for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Albendazole","D":"Doxycycline","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult worms 15 to 35 cm long passed from the rectum, with eggs in stool, diagnose Ascaris lumbricoides. Katzung lists albendazole, pyrantel pamoate, or mebendazole as the drugs of choice. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris has knobby-coated oval eggs in feces and is treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each adult female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day into the stool (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole are thought to kill nematodes by inhibiting what?","choices":{"A":"Ergosterol synthesis","B":"Folate synthesis","C":"Peptidoglycan synthesis","D":"Chitin synthesis","E":"Microtubule synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Benzimidazoles, including mebendazole and albendazole, are thought to act against nematodes by inhibiting microtubule synthesis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mebendazole binds tubulin and is listed with the microtubule-acting drugs (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: less than 10% of an oral mebendazole dose is absorbed (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibiotic kills adult filarial worms indirectly by eliminating their Wolbachia bacterial symbionts?","choices":{"A":"Doxycycline","B":"Azithromycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Vancomycin","E":"Clindamycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Doxycycline has macrofilaricidal activity against Wuchereria bancrofti and activity against onchocerciasis because it kills Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial symbiont of filarial parasites. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Doxycycline). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and are generally avoided in pregnancy and in children, except doxycycline (First Aid Antimicrobials — Tetracyclines). COMLEX: Katzung reports better activity against adult W bancrofti than any other available drug (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes by increasing their membrane permeability to which ion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride","B":"Sodium","C":"Calcium","D":"Potassium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death of the worm. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia infections (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel tablets are swallowed without chewing because the bitter taste can induce retching (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice is used for lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and for loiasis?","choices":{"A":"Diethylcarbamazine","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Piperazine citrate","E":"Niclosamide tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diethylcarbamazine is a drug of choice for W bancrofti, Brugia, and Loa loa because of its efficacy and lack of serious toxicity; it immobilizes microfilariae and exposes them to host defenses. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa (deer fly) and Wuchereria bancrofti (mosquito) are both treated with diethylcarbamazine (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: the plasma half-life of diethylcarbamazine is longer in alkaline urine (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For neurocysticercosis, why is albendazole now generally preferred over praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Avoids need for corticosteroids","B":"Single oral dose regimen","C":"No liver toxicity with long use","D":"Better subarachnoid penetration","E":"Proven safety in early pregnancy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albendazole is preferred for neurocysticercosis because of a shorter course, lower cost, improved penetration into the subarachnoid space, and higher drug levels with corticosteroids. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole, while intestinal taeniasis is treated with praziquantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: corticosteroids are usually given with the antihelminthic to limit inflammation from dying organisms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For uncomplicated strongyloidiasis, which drug is given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Niclosamide","D":"Oxamniquine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days; thiabendazole is an alternative but is more toxic. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is treated with ivermectin or bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: in disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients, monthly suppressive therapy may help (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old sheep farmer with a large hepatic hydatid cyst starts several months of albendazole before surgery. Which monitoring is advised during this long course?","choices":{"A":"Blood counts and liver tests","B":"Serum creatinine and urinalysis","C":"Serial ECG for QT changes","D":"Audiometry and visual fields","E":"Serum magnesium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-term albendazole for hydatid disease is usually well tolerated but can cause raised liver enzymes and pancytopenia, so blood counts and liver function are monitored. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Echinococcus hydatid cysts are treated with albendazole, with surgery for some cysts (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole should be avoided in patients with cirrhosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks' gestation has perianal itching, and a tape test shows pinworm eggs. Her physician defers mebendazole. What property of mebendazole explains this?","choices":{"A":"Causes neonatal gray syndrome","B":"Inhibits fetal folate uptake","C":"Causes fetal tooth staining","D":"Teratogenic in animal studies","E":"Causes fetal renal tubule damage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mebendazole is teratogenic in animals and is therefore contraindicated in pregnancy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: for pinworm, mebendazole 100 mg is given once and repeated at 2 weeks (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from Ghana with onchocerciasis receives a single oral dose of the drug of choice, a macrocyclic lactone. Concurrent use of which drug class should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Beta-blockers","D":"ACE inhibitors","E":"Benzodiazepines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so it is best not combined with other GABA-enhancing drugs such as barbiturates, benzodiazepines, or valproic acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus causes black skin nodules and river blindness (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: treatment reactions in onchocerciasis come mainly from killing microfilariae and peak on day 2 (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Cameroon has a worm crossing his conjunctiva and a very high Loa loa microfilarial load, well above the 2500/mL high-risk level. Which initial approach is safest?","choices":{"A":"Immediate full-dose diethylcarbamazine","B":"Single-dose praziquantel","C":"Slower-acting albendazole first","D":"Topical thiabendazole cream","E":"Weekly oral niclosamide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For heavy Loa loa burdens (more than 2500 parasites/mL), Katzung lists apheresis or therapy with albendazole, which is slower acting and better tolerated, before definitive filaricidal treatment. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate; Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa causes skin swelling and a worm in the conjunctiva (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: reactions to dying microfilariae are occasionally severe in L loa infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with symptomatic parenchymal neurocysticercosis is taking dexamethasone for cerebral edema. What effect does dexamethasone have on praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Reduced plasma drug levels","B":"Increased plasma drug levels","C":"Prolonged elimination half-life","D":"Greater CSF drug penetration","E":"Faster cyst calcification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corticosteroids can decrease plasma praziquantel levels by up to 50%, whereas albendazole levels increase, one reason albendazole is preferred when steroids are needed. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium cysticercosis follows ingestion of eggs in fecally contaminated food (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: some workers caution against praziquantel in spinal neurocysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from Peru has right upper quadrant pain, eosinophilia, and Fasciola hepatica eggs after eating watercress. Why is praziquantel a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Excessive toxicity in hepatic disease","B":"Lack of activity in this infection","C":"Poor oral bioavailability in adults","D":"Need for prolonged IV dosing","E":"High rates of liver fluke relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Praziquantel treats most trematodes but was not effective for fascioliasis even at high doses; triclabendazole is the drug of choice, with bithionol as an alternative. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Triclabendazole; Bithionol). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats trematodes such as Schistosoma and Clonorchis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: triclabendazole may prolong the QT interval (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Metoclopramide speeds gastric emptying and raises lower esophageal sphincter pressure mainly by what action?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic M3 receptor agonism","B":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade","D":"Histamine H2 receptor blockade","E":"Mu opioid receptor agonism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist; blocking inhibitory dopamine receptors in the gut raises LES pressure, enhances esophageal peristalsis and speeds gastric emptying, with little effect on small bowel or colon. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist used for diabetic gastroparesis and persistent GERD (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide also blocks D2 receptors in the area postrema chemoreceptor trigger zone (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent given intravenously is used to treat acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)?","choices":{"A":"Atropine","B":"Octreotide","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Dicyclomine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine at enteric synapses; a 2 mg IV dose rapidly evacuates colonic gas and stool in acute pseudo-obstruction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neostigmine is a quaternary (charged) AChE inhibitor that does not enter the CNS and treats postoperative ileus and urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: cholinergic effects of the IV dose include salivation, vomiting and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does domperidone cause extrapyramidal effects far less often than metoclopramide?","choices":{"A":"Poor blood-brain barrier entry","B":"Faster renal clearance of the drug","C":"Weaker binding at gut D2 receptors","D":"Added serotonin 5-HT4 agonism","E":"Greater pituitary prolactin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Domperidone shares the D2-blocking mechanism but crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central extrapyramidal effects are rare. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: centrally acting D2 blockers cause parkinsonism and acute dystonia as drug-induced movement disorders (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone still acts at the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Alosetron use for diarrhea-predominant IBS is restricted mainly because of what serious adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Torsades de pointes","B":"Ischemic colitis","C":"Agranulocytosis","D":"QT shortening","E":"Hepatic necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, causes constipation in up to 30% and ischemic colitis in up to 3 per 1000 patients, so it is restricted to women with severe IBS with diarrhea. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are antiemetics whose adverse effects include headache and constipation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on extrinsic afferents that carry visceral pain signals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Lubiprostone relieves constipation-predominant IBS by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2 chloride channel activation","B":"Mu opioid receptor activation in gut","C":"Na+/K+ ATPase pump inhibition","D":"Bulk retention of stool water","E":"5-HT3 receptor inhibition on afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lubiprostone, a prostanoic acid derivative, activates type 2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical enterocyte membrane, increasing chloride-rich fluid secretion into the lumen. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Chloride Channel Activator). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lubiprostone appears in the anticonstipation table with diarrhea and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: lubiprostone is approved for women with IBS-C and avoided in women who may become pregnant (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Eluxadoline for diarrhea-predominant IBS should be avoided in a patient with which history?","choices":{"A":"Prior appendectomy","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Prior pancreatitis","D":"Recurrent gout attacks","E":"Migraine headaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eluxadoline, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause sphincter of Oddi spasm with pancreatitis, so it is avoided with prior pancreatitis, alcoholism or sphincter of Oddi disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripherally acting mu agonists slow gut motility with low addiction potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a lower 75 mg dose is used after cholecystectomy because sphincter of Oddi complications are more common (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide is an analog of which gut hormone?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon-like peptide 1","B":"Glucose insulinotropic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Glucagon-like peptide 2","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog used for short-bowel syndrome; GLP-2 is an intestinotrophic peptide released from L cells. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs such as exenatide are incretin drugs, unlike the intestinotrophic analog used for short gut (First Aid Endocrine Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide binds receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"By which route is teduglutide given for short-bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Weekly intravenous infusion pump","B":"Twice-daily oral tablet","C":"Monthly intramuscular depot","D":"Daily rectal suppository","E":"Daily subcutaneous injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teduglutide is given at 0.05 mg/kg once daily by subcutaneous injection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog given by subcutaneous injection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: teduglutide dosing is weight based at 0.05 mg/kg per day (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide helps patients with short-bowel syndrome mainly by promoting what?","choices":{"A":"Faster emptying of the stomach","B":"Slower colonic transit","C":"Mucosal epithelial growth","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output","E":"Hepatic bile acid output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Through trophic hormones, teduglutide stimulates mucosal epithelial growth of the remaining bowel and enhances fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: short-bowel syndrome causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluid after extensive resection (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide acts on enteric neurons and endocrine cells to adapt the residual gut (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for 2 years. She now has repetitive lip smacking and tongue thrusting that persist after the drug is stopped. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonic reaction","B":"Akathisia restlessness","C":"Serotonin syndrome","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Essential tremor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-term metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia that may be irreversible, so prolonged use is avoided, especially in older adults. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed orofacial choreiform movements follow long-term D2 blockade by antipsychotics or prokinetics (First Aid Pharmacology (drug adverse effects)). COMLEX: tardive movements are more likely with long-term use in older patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes metoclopramide for postsurgical gastroparesis. Months later she has milky nipple discharge and missed periods. Which laboratory finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum prolactin","B":"High serum cortisol","C":"High serum prolactin","D":"Low free thyroxine level","E":"High serum calcium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release; D2 blockade raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea, amenorrhea and gynecomastia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine antagonists are a drug cause of hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone is used off-label for postpartum lactation through the same pituitary effect (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with diabetic gastroparesis improves on oral erythromycin, which induces fasting migrating motor complexes. After several weeks the benefit fades despite the same dose. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid breakdown of the drug","B":"Rapid development of tolerance","C":"Induced CYP3A4 metabolism","D":"Selection of resistant flora","E":"Worsening autonomic damage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and initiates the migrating motor complex, which normally recurs about every 90 minutes during fasting; tolerance develops rapidly, limiting long-term use. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Macrolides). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 and cause QT prolongation as notable interactions (First Aid Antimicrobials (macrolides)). COMLEX: a single IV macrolide dose of 3 mg/kg can clear the stomach of blood before upper endoscopy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — migrating motor complex during fasting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has IBS with diarrhea: frequent loose stools and urgency but mild pain. She wants an inexpensive over-the-counter medicine. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Psyllium","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Neomycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loperamide, a peripherally acting mu-opioid agonist, reduces stool frequency and urgency in IBS with diarrhea; it does not relieve abdominal pain. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antidiarrheal mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and cause constipation and nausea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to discourage overdosage (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has IBS with constipation and bloating. Fiber and over-the-counter laxatives have not helped. He wants a capsule taken each morning that raises enterocyte cGMP. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Lubiprostone","B":"Linaclotide","C":"Alosetron","D":"Eluxadoline","E":"Hyoscyamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linaclotide (290 mcg daily) activates guanylyl cyclase C, raising cGMP, which opens CFTR and increases chloride and bicarbonate secretion; lubiprostone is approved in women. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Guanylate Cyclase C Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GC-C agonists such as plecanatide cause diarrhea, bloating and flatulence (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GC-C agonists are contraindicated in children because of dehydration risk (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has mixed-type IBS, alternating stool form with chronic abdominal pain as her main complaint. Which nightly drug best targets the pain?","choices":{"A":"High-dose bismuth salts","B":"Daily lactulose syrup by mouth","C":"Oral metoclopramide","D":"Low-dose amitriptyline","E":"Weekly erythromycin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants alter central processing of visceral afferent input and help chronic IBS pain; their anticholinergic effect also slows stools. Katzung does not separate mixed-type therapy, so this item is objectives-first. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tricyclic antidepressants cause anticholinergic, sedative and cardiac conduction effects (First Aid Psychiatry Pharmacology). COMLEX: desipramine is dosed at 10 to 50 mg per day for chronic IBS pain (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had massive small-bowel resection after a volvulus and needs home intravenous nutrition 6 nights a week. Teduglutide is started. Which outcome best shows that it is working?","choices":{"A":"Higher fasting glucose","B":"Faster gastric emptying","C":"Lower serum prolactin level","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin","E":"Reduced infusion frequency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In trials, 54% of patients given teduglutide cut parenteral support by at least 1 day per week, versus 23% given placebo. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extensive resection removes absorptive surface and causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluids (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: patients with under 200 cm of small intestine usually need partial or complete parenteral support (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome is being considered for teduglutide. Which screening is most important because of a known drug risk?","choices":{"A":"Echocardiography for valves","B":"Bone density scanning","C":"Colonoscopy for neoplasia","D":"Audiometry for hearing","E":"Slit-lamp eye examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teduglutide is trophic to the mucosa and may raise the risk of colorectal and small-bowel neoplasia, so colonoscopy is done before and during therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trophic stimulation of intestinal epithelium raises concern for adenomatous growth (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: GLP-2 analog therapy carries a neoplasia warning for colon and small bowel (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome after Crohn resections starts teduglutide. Its effect on the remaining mucosa is mediated downstream by release of which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-like growth factor","B":"Histamine from mast cells","C":"Somatostatin from D cells","D":"Substance P from neurons","E":"Gastrin from antral G cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells and stimulates release of trophic hormones, including insulin-like growth factor, that drive epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP from enteric neurons increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Physiology (GI regulatory substances)). COMLEX: octreotide may be used for high-output diarrhea in short-bowel syndrome (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Docusate softens stool mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Lowering surface tension","B":"Drawing water in by osmosis","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel","E":"Opening apical ion channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Docusate is a stool surfactant that lets water and lipids penetrate the stool; it is often given to hospitalized patients to minimize straining. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emollient laxatives are surfactants that promote water entry into stool (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: glycerin suppositories are another stool surfactant option given rectally (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"A woman has used an anthraquinone laxative daily for years. Which colonoscopy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestone mucosa","C":"Melanosis coli","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aloe, senna and cascara are hydrolyzed in the colon; chronic use causes brown colonic pigmentation called melanosis coli, without a proven link to colorectal cancer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anthraquinone Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives act by enteric nerve stimulation and colonic contraction, with diarrhea as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: oral anthraquinones act in 6 to 12 hours and rectal doses within 2 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term use of mineral oil as a lubricant laxative can cause which nutritional problem?","choices":{"A":"Copper deficiency anemia","B":"Iron overload in the liver","C":"Zinc deficiency rash","D":"Fat-soluble vitamin loss","E":"Excess vitamin C levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and retards water absorption; long-term use impairs absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, and aspiration can cause lipid pneumonitis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K depends on bile emulsification, and mineral oil intake can cause their deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, fat-soluble vitamins). COMLEX: mineral oil is used for fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do diphenoxylate tablets contain a small amount of atropine?","choices":{"A":"Enhancing pain relief","B":"Preventing nausea","C":"Discouraging abuse","D":"Delaying tolerance","E":"Speeding absorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diphenoxylate is a prescription opioid agonist with CNS effects and dependence at higher doses; 0.025 mg atropine is added to discourage overdosage and may add to the antidiarrheal action. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diphenoxylate is a mu agonist that slows gut motility with constipation and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: opioids slow colonic transit by inhibiting presynaptic cholinergic nerves in the enteric plexuses (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term somatostatin-analog therapy for acromegaly most commonly causes which problem?","choices":{"A":"Kidney calcium oxalate stones","B":"Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia","C":"Sustained sinus tachycardia","D":"Hyperthyroid storm crisis","E":"Gallbladder sludge or stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting gallbladder contractility and altering fat absorption, long-term octreotide causes sludge or gallstones in over 50% of patients; it can also cause steatorrhea, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism and bradycardia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs raise cholelithiasis risk through CCK inhibition (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a monthly intramuscular depot form of the somatostatin analog is available (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Colestipol for chronic diarrhea should be taken at least 2 hours apart from other medicines. Why?","choices":{"A":"It induces hepatic CYP3A4","B":"It binds many oral drugs","C":"It raises gastric pH","D":"It blocks P-glycoprotein","E":"It speeds gastric emptying"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholestyramine and colestipol bind many drugs and reduce their absorption, so they are separated from other drugs by 2 hours; colesevelam has little effect on drug absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins cause GI upset and decreased absorption of other drugs (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin adverse effects include bloating, flatulence, constipation and fecal impaction (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Sulfasalazine releases active 5-aminosalicylic acid in the colon through which process?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial azoreductase cleavage","B":"Hepatic CYP450 oxidation of it","C":"Gastric acid hydrolysis of the bond","D":"Pancreatic esterase cleavage","E":"pH-dependent resin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine by an azo bond; colonic bacteria cleave it with azoreductase, releasing 5-ASA in the terminal ileum and colon. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine combines antibacterial sulfapyridine with anti-inflammatory 5-ASA and is activated by colonic bacteria (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: mesalamine products instead use timed-release granules or pH-sensitive coatings (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient starting sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis should take which daily supplement?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Vitamin D3","C":"Iron sulfate","D":"Folic acid","E":"Zinc salts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so 1 mg/d folic acid is recommended. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine adverse effects include malaise, nausea, sulfonamide toxicity and reversible oligospermia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: slow acetylators of sulfapyridine have more frequent and severe adverse effects (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Before starting azathioprine for Crohn disease, which test helps predict severe marrow toxicity?","choices":{"A":"G6PD enzyme activity","B":"TPMT enzyme activity","C":"HLA-B*5701 typing","D":"Serum ferritin level","E":"CYP2C19 genotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiopurine methyltransferase catabolizes 6-MP; activity is low in 11% and absent in 0.3% of people, raising active thioguanine nucleotides and marrow suppression risk. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine is an antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine that causes pancytopenia (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: complete blood counts and liver tests are monitored in every patient on purine analogs (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has taken milk of magnesia nightly for months. He now has weakness, absent reflexes and bradycardia. Which disturbance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypokalemia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hyperphosphatemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium hydroxide should not be used for long periods in renal insufficiency because of the risk of hypermagnesemia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium-containing laxatives provide an osmotic load and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the colon neither concentrates nor dilutes fecal fluid, so nonabsorbed solute obligates stool water (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman with mild renal insufficiency takes sodium phosphate tablets for colonoscopy preparation. A week later her creatinine has doubled. Biopsy would most likely show which change?","choices":{"A":"Urate nephropathy","B":"Nephrocalcinosis","C":"Oxalate nephropathy","D":"Myoglobin casts","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium phosphate can cause hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, and acute renal failure from tubular calcium phosphate deposition (nephrocalcinosis); it is avoided in frail, elderly or renal patients. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic agents draw water into the lumen and can be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: balanced isotonic lavage solutions avoid intravascular fluid and electrolyte shifts during preparation (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old bed-bound nursing home resident has had no bowel movement for 4 days. Staff want a stimulant laxative that works within an hour. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral senna tablets","B":"Oral psyllium powder","C":"Oral docusate capsule","D":"Bisacodyl suppository","E":"Oral lactulose syrup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bisacodyl, a diphenylmethane stimulant, works in 30 to 60 minutes rectally versus 6 to 10 hours orally, with minimal systemic absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Diphenylmethane Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives cause colonic contraction through enteric nerve stimulation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: long-term stimulant use is probably safe and may be needed in neurologically impaired patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. He now has watery postprandial diarrhea without steatorrhea. Which drug best treats it?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Pancrelipase","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Diphenoxylate","E":"Metronidazole tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal disease or resection causes malabsorbed bile salts that trigger colonic secretory diarrhea; bile salt-binding resins reduce it, though they can worsen fat malabsorption if the bile pool is depleted. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins prevent intestinal reabsorption of bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin powders are taken one to three times daily before meals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with metastatic carcinoid tumor has flushing, wheezing and voluminous watery diarrhea. Her liver lesions cannot be removed surgically. Which drug best controls her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate","B":"Octreotide","C":"Bismuth","D":"Lubiprostone","E":"Prednisone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide inhibits hormone secretion by carcinoid and VIPoma tumors, reducing secretory diarrhea and flushing, and may slow tumor progression. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome is diagnosed by urinary 5-HIAA and treated with a somatostatin analog (First Aid Endocrine, carcinoid syndrome). COMLEX: higher subcutaneous doses of 100 to 250 mcg inhibit motility in dumping or vagotomy diarrhea (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, a temperature of 39.4°C and abdominal tenderness after eating undercooked chicken. Why should loperamide be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Risk of central opioid dependence","B":"Risk of respiratory depression in adults","C":"Risk of severe serotonin syndrome","D":"Risk of worsened underlying illness","E":"Risk of tardive movement disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antidiarrheals are safe for mild to moderate acute diarrhea but should not be used with bloody diarrhea, high fever or systemic toxicity because they may worsen the underlying condition. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and low addictive potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: antidiarrheals are stopped when diarrhea worsens despite therapy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease is maintained on azathioprine. She is started on allopurinol for gout and 3 weeks later has a white cell count of 1,100/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). What explains this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal drug excretion","B":"Increased drug absorption","C":"Reduced 6-MP breakdown","D":"Allopurinol marrow allergy","E":"Folate antagonism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase catabolism of the purine analogs, increasing active 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe leukopenia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6-MP is degraded by xanthine oxidase, so its toxicity rises with allopurinol (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: febuxostat raises toxicity the same way, and purine-analog benefit in IBD has a median delay of 17 weeks because thioguanine nucleotides accumulate slowly (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease who emigrated from the Philippines will begin infliximab. Which test must be done first?","choices":{"A":"Latent tuberculosis test","B":"Serum ceruloplasmin level","C":"Hepatitis A antibody titer","D":"Stool culture panel","E":"Repeat colonic biopsy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-TNF therapy suppresses the TH1 response; all patients need a tuberculin skin test or interferon gamma release assay first, and positive patients get prophylaxis before therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF is important in granuloma formation and stabilization, and anti-TNF drugs can cause drug-induced lupus (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: serious infection risk rises markedly with concurrent corticosteroids (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has mild to moderate Crohn disease of the ileum and ascending colon. She wants a glucocorticoid with fewer systemic adverse effects. Which is best?","choices":{"A":"Oral prednisone at high dose","B":"Hydrocortisone retention enema","C":"IV methylprednisolone","D":"Oral dexamethasone daily","E":"Controlled-release budesonide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budesonide has high glucocorticoid receptor affinity but extensive CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism; the distal ileum and colon release form treats mild to moderate ileocolonic Crohn disease with less systemic toxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inactivate NF-kB and inhibit synthesis of almost all cytokines (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: potent CYP3A4 inhibitors raise plasma levels of the controlled-release steroid severalfold (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP exerts its main cytotoxic effect by binding which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase","C":"DNA polymerase alpha","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"Thymidine kinase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking de novo thymidylate synthesis (\"thymineless death\"); FUTP and FdUTP are also incorporated into RNA and DNA, and the drug acts in S phase. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog bioactivated to 5-FdUMP, lowering dTMP and DNA synthesis, and its effects are enhanced by adding leucovorin (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: 5-FU is given IV because it is broken down rapidly in the GI tract, and its plasma half-life is about 10 to 15 minutes (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving FOLFOX for stage III colon cancer notices tingling in his fingers and throat when he holds a chilled drink during the infusion. Which toxicity is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute sensory neurotoxicity","B":"Cumulative motor axonopathy","C":"Cryoglobulin vasculitis flare","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany episode","E":"Cerebellar ataxia toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The platinum agent in FOLFOX causes dose-limiting peripheral sensory neuropathy in two forms: an acute form triggered and worsened by cold, and a chronic cumulative dose-dependent form that is more readily reversible than cisplatin neuropathy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, are cell cycle nonspecific, and cause peripheral neuropathy, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: FOLFOX is the most widely used first-line regimen for metastatic colorectal cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"Before cetuximab is added for metastatic colorectal cancer, the tumor must show which molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"HER2 gene amplification","B":"Wild-type KRAS and NRAS","C":"High EGFR protein expression","D":"BRAF V600E mutation","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR; its efficacy is restricted to tumors with wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), because a mutant RAS signals downstream of the blocked receptor. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies cause rash, elevated LFTs, and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: because cetuximab is IgG1, part of its antitumor effect may be immune mediated (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which orally available prodrug is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase within tumor tissue?","choices":{"A":"Gemcitabine","B":"Cytarabine","C":"Methotrexate","D":"Hydroxyurea","E":"Capecitabine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Capecitabine has 70% to 80% oral bioavailability; liver carboxylesterase and cytidine deaminase form 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which thymidine phosphorylase (high in colorectal tumors) converts to 5-FU. It is paired with oxaliplatin as CAPOX. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine is the prodrug of 5-FU listed with the pyrimidine antimetabolites (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: CAPOX is used first line in metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which DNA cross-linking agent has myelosuppression, with much less renal and GI toxicity than cisplatin, as its main dose-limiting effect?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Carboplatin","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Busulfan"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carboplatin shares the mechanism, resistance, and spectrum of cisplatin, but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression with significantly less renal and GI toxicity, so it is easier to give and has replaced cisplatin in many regimens. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, and cisplatin nephrotoxicity can be prevented with amifostine (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: platinum agents bind mainly the N7 position of guanine and work in all cell cycle phases (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anti-EGFR antibody is a fully human IgG2 with fewer infusion reactions?","choices":{"A":"Panitumumab","B":"Cetuximab","C":"Necitumumab","D":"Trastuzumab","E":"Pertuzumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Panitumumab is a fully human IgG2 anti-EGFR antibody, so it is not expected to have immune-mediated effects, and infusion reactions are much less common than with chimeric cetuximab; acneiform rash is a main adverse effect. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -umab ending marks a human monoclonal antibody and -ximab a chimeric human-mouse one (First Aid Pharmacology, biologic agents). COMLEX: necitumumab is an IgG1 anti-EGFR antibody used for squamous NSCLC and carries thromboembolic risk (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage III colon cancer gets her first cycle of an IV fluoropyrimidine with leucovorin and a platinum agent. Within a week she has severe mouth ulcers, profuse diarrhea, and confusion; absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference 1,500–8,000/µL). Deficiency of which enzyme best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Thiopurine methyltransferase","B":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","C":"Uridine glucuronosyltransferase","D":"Hypoxanthine phosphoribosyl transferase","E":"Cytidine deaminase enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most of an administered 5-FU dose is catabolized by DPD. Partial or complete DPD deficiency, an autosomal recessive trait seen in up to 5% of cancer patients (Katzung Ch. 54 elsewhere cites up to 10% of the North American population), causes severe toxicity with the triad of myelosuppression, GI toxicity (diarrhea, mucositis), and neurotoxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU causes myelosuppression and is used for colon and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: not every DPD-deficient patient presents with this classic triad (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man receives FOLFIRI for metastatic colon cancer. Two hours into the infusion he has abdominal cramps, watery stools, sweating, and salivation. Which drug best treats this early reaction?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Atropine","D":"Ondansetron","E":"Metoclopramide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Irinotecan causes two forms of diarrhea: an early form within 24 hours that is a cholinergic event effectively treated with atropine, and a late form 2 to 10 days later that may cause severe electrolyte imbalance and dehydration. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: irinotecan and topotecan block topoisomerase I and cause severe myelosuppression and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: irinotecan and its metabolite are cleared mainly in bile and feces, so liver dysfunction requires a dose reduction (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic nonseminomatous testicular cancer receives cisplatin, bleomycin, and a semisynthetic mayapple-root derivative. The derivative acts mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Topoisomerase II blockade","B":"Topoisomerase I blockade","C":"Microtubule stabilization","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking","E":"DNA polymerase blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin from the mayapple root, complexes with topoisomerase II and DNA, preventing religation of double-strand breaks and impairing DNA synthesis. It is active in germ cell and lung cancers; renal dysfunction needs dose reduction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: topoisomerase II inhibitors cause myelosuppression and alopecia and treat testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: topoisomerase inhibitors arrest cells in the S and G2 phases (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with metastatic colorectal cancer is starting second-line therapy. Her oncologist adds to FOLFIRI a humanized antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A. Which drug was added?","choices":{"A":"Cetuximab","B":"Necitumumab","C":"Trastuzumab","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Rituximab"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bevacizumab binds all forms of VEGF-A and keeps it from reaching VEGF receptors; it combines with 5-FU-, irinotecan-, and oxaliplatin-based therapy in metastatic colorectal cancer; unlike the anti-EGFR antibodies, it targets the ligand rather than EGFR. Hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, GI perforation, and proteinuria are key risks. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anti-VEGF antibody also treats renal cell carcinoma, NSCLC, and angioproliferative retinopathy, with hemorrhage and blood clots as adverse effects (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: it does not appear to worsen the usual toxicities of cytotoxic chemotherapy (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has metastatic colon cancer whose cells lack mismatch repair function and resist the older platinum drugs in culture. Which DNA cross-linking agent is least affected by this resistance?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Busulfan","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Oxaliplatin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tumors resistant to cisplatin or carboplatin through mismatch repair defects are not cross-resistant to oxaliplatin, which is used with 5-FU and leucovorin (FOLFOX) in metastatic and adjuvant colorectal cancer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds treat GI, testicular, bladder, ovarian, and lung tumors (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: oxaliplatin regimens are also active in pancreatic, gastroesophageal, and hepatocellular cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man on an anti-EGFR antibody plus FOLFOX for metastatic colorectal cancer develops an acneiform facial rash, muscle cramps, and tremor. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum magnesium","B":"High serum potassium","C":"Low serum sodium","D":"High serum calcium","E":"Low serum phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypomagnesemia and acneiform skin rash are the two main adverse effects shared by cetuximab and panitumumab; neuromuscular irritability such as cramps and tremor fits low magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: cetuximab given with radiation for head and neck cancer carries a small (1%) sudden-death risk and a black-box warning (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true}]};
